1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:17,283
We're here with two actors,
Namkoong Min and Kim Dae-myung,

2
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:19,919
deeply adored by many viewers.

3
00:00:19,986 --> 00:00:22,088
One of our own chefs

4
00:00:22,155 --> 00:00:23,990
was in a scene with Dae-myung

5
00:00:24,057 --> 00:00:27,961
-and showed off his master acting skills.
-They acted?

6
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:29,329
-Someone acted?
-Really?

7
00:00:29,396 --> 00:00:31,731
-Master skills?
-Who was it?

8
00:00:31,798 --> 00:00:34,968
Chef Sam and I

9
00:00:35,035 --> 00:00:39,205
were in the music video "THINK OF YOU"
by Young-jun of Brown Eyed Soul.

10
00:00:39,472 --> 00:00:41,541
SAM KIM, AN ACTOR?

11
00:00:41,608 --> 00:00:44,110
I was the owner of a tofu restaurant,

12
00:00:44,177 --> 00:00:46,813
and he played a regular customer there.

13
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:48,415
-The customer?
-You were the customer?

14
00:00:48,481 --> 00:00:50,050
You acted as the customer?

15
00:00:50,116 --> 00:00:51,851
Isn't it supposed to be
the other way around?

16
00:00:51,918 --> 00:00:54,387
We got our hands on that music video.

17
00:00:54,454 --> 00:00:55,955
WE GOT THE MUSIC VIDEO

18
00:00:56,022 --> 00:00:57,991
This is "THINK OF YOU" by Young-jun.

19
00:00:58,058 --> 00:00:59,459
-No way.
-Let's watch it.

20
00:00:59,526 --> 00:01:02,695
He was in it with Dae-myung.
See? There he is.

21
00:01:02,762 --> 00:01:04,097
<i>Tofu delivery.</i>

22
00:01:04,164 --> 00:01:05,298
Yes, it was tofu.

23
00:01:05,365 --> 00:01:06,933
FELL IN LOVE WITH THE DELIVERY WOMAN

24
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,168
She was there to deliver tofu?

25
00:01:08,234 --> 00:01:09,769
He wants to see her again.

26
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:12,405
It's Sam!

27
00:01:12,472 --> 00:01:13,740
SAM KIM, A REGULAR CUSTOMER

28
00:01:13,807 --> 00:01:15,809
Who does that to order more rice?

29
00:01:15,875 --> 00:01:17,143
-Oh, no.
-Is he having bean rice?

30
00:01:17,343 --> 00:01:19,646
MORE TOFU, NOT RICE

31
00:01:21,247 --> 00:01:23,416
DOING HIS BEST TO SHOW A DUMBFOUNDED FACE

32
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:25,218
EVERYTHING IS TOFU

33
00:01:25,285 --> 00:01:26,586
<i>They're all tofu dishes.</i>

34
00:01:26,653 --> 00:01:29,289
<i>That guy keeps coming back.</i>
<i>He is a regular there.</i>

35
00:01:29,355 --> 00:01:33,626
SAM SHOWED HIS MASTER SKILLS AS AN ACTOR
IN THE SAME SCENE WITH DAE-MYUNG

36
00:01:33,693 --> 00:01:35,728
It was a prop,
so it probably tasted bad too.

37
00:01:35,929 --> 00:01:37,931
BLUSH

38
00:01:38,932 --> 00:01:41,401
-That was fun.
-He wasn't all that awkward.

39
00:01:41,468 --> 00:01:44,771
-I can see now that I was quite talented.
-Talented in what?

40
00:01:45,004 --> 00:01:46,739
YOU'RE RIGHT

41
00:01:46,806 --> 00:01:48,975
Did you two meet
for the first time back then?

42
00:01:49,042 --> 00:01:50,710
-Yes.
-It was the first time.

43
00:01:50,777 --> 00:01:52,512
What was his first impression?

44
00:01:52,579 --> 00:01:56,182
There are many actors
who are intimidatingly charismatic, right?

45
00:01:56,249 --> 00:01:57,951
But he seemed more like my kind--

46
00:01:58,017 --> 00:01:59,886
No, he isn't.

47
00:01:59,953 --> 00:02:03,223
On the other hand, there are chefs
who are flashy and loud, aren't there?

48
00:02:03,523 --> 00:02:05,725
THE FLASHY GUY

49
00:02:06,392 --> 00:02:07,560
He's flashy.

50
00:02:07,627 --> 00:02:10,697
-So he's more like you than Hyun-suk?
-That's it.

51
00:02:10,763 --> 00:02:13,032
He seemed like the calmer type.

52
00:02:13,099 --> 00:02:15,301
He's finally using his canine
to chew me out.

53
00:02:15,368 --> 00:02:18,271
-He's starting to attack Hyun-suk.
-He's finally chewing out.

54
00:02:18,338 --> 00:02:20,306
So he thinks Dae-myung is like him.

55
00:02:20,373 --> 00:02:22,542
We actually received a tip
regarding Dae-myung.

56
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:25,645
He's famous as a foodie
in the entertainment business.

57
00:02:25,712 --> 00:02:30,083
Also, he creates foodie content
under his own name,

58
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:33,553
titled <i>Food, Vibe, Dae-myung</i>.

59
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:34,754
<i>How did that begin?</i>

60
00:02:34,821 --> 00:02:36,189
-FVD.
-When I meet

61
00:02:36,256 --> 00:02:39,058
with writers or friends
that I'm close to,

62
00:02:39,125 --> 00:02:41,694
we never drink a lot of soju
in just one place.

63
00:02:41,761 --> 00:02:43,363
We have a beer somewhere,

64
00:02:43,429 --> 00:02:45,932
then walk for about a kilometer
and have a drink of soju.

65
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:49,602
So I end up hopping
seven or eight times a day.

66
00:02:49,669 --> 00:02:51,471
ALWAYS RESTAURANT-HOPS 7 TO 8 TIMES

67
00:02:51,538 --> 00:02:53,773
It's like going on a day trip.

68
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:55,175
You go anywhere that catches your eye?

69
00:02:55,241 --> 00:02:59,145
What's this about?
Your greatest rival is sitting right here?

70
00:02:59,212 --> 00:03:00,513
Your greatest rival is here?

71
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,382
-Who's your rival?
-There is someone

72
00:03:02,448 --> 00:03:03,917
-I've noticed recently…
-I think I know.

73
00:03:03,983 --> 00:03:05,752
-…as a YouTuber.
-Huh?

74
00:03:05,818 --> 00:03:07,754
-Who is it?
-It is

75
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:11,291
-Chef Nam-no.
-Yoon Nam-no!

76
00:03:11,357 --> 00:03:13,159
He's been eating so much lately.

77
00:03:13,226 --> 00:03:15,628
I watched him and realized
he and I are quite alike.

78
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:17,197
-We'd hit it off.
-Really?

79
00:03:17,263 --> 00:03:20,300
You can tell whether a food show
is scripted or not, right?

80
00:03:20,366 --> 00:03:21,834
Right. You can tell.

81
00:03:21,901 --> 00:03:25,805
I can tell when it's genuine,
and you actually disclosed your favorites.

82
00:03:25,872 --> 00:03:27,640
I only introduce places I've been to.

83
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,042
ME TOO!

84
00:03:29,108 --> 00:03:30,410
And I also hop six, seven times.

85
00:03:30,476 --> 00:03:31,611
Six, seven times?

86
00:03:31,678 --> 00:03:33,913
I like to eat lightly
in many different places

87
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:36,249
-for over six to seven hours.
-I see.

88
00:03:36,316 --> 00:03:38,051
That's not for me.

89
00:03:38,451 --> 00:03:41,020
HAS A CLEAR PREFERENCE

90
00:03:41,087 --> 00:03:42,522
You stick to one place.

91
00:03:42,589 --> 00:03:45,058
-I like to just stay in one place forever.
-Yes, just staying put.

92
00:03:45,124 --> 00:03:47,827
-It's too much of a hassle.
-Right? Why would you do that?

93
00:03:47,894 --> 00:03:50,296
-Everything's there already.
-It's already so cozy.

94
00:03:50,363 --> 00:03:51,631
-Just…
-In one place forever.

95
00:03:51,698 --> 00:03:54,834
Min, do you like
looking for good restaurants?

96
00:03:54,901 --> 00:03:59,072
I often make reservations through an app,

97
00:03:59,138 --> 00:04:03,276
and I also enjoy finding
and dining at new joints.

98
00:04:03,343 --> 00:04:04,611
Do you make reservations yourself

99
00:04:04,677 --> 00:04:06,112
on the phone?

100
00:04:06,179 --> 00:04:08,081
No, these days, there's an app.

101
00:04:08,147 --> 00:04:10,650
-Who makes calls these days?
-Go on the internet. Please.

102
00:04:10,717 --> 00:04:13,553
-Who makes calls?
-Go online for once.

103
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:15,154
Even I use apps.

104
00:04:15,788 --> 00:04:18,891
"Hi, I'm Kim Sung-ju.
Can I make a reservation?"

105
00:04:18,958 --> 00:04:21,160
-"Who are you?"
-Wait.

106
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:23,429
-I don't really use apps.
-Do you make calls?

107
00:04:23,496 --> 00:04:25,031
-I do.
-What do you say?

108
00:04:25,098 --> 00:04:28,067
-I ask if they have a spot.
-"Hi, you've reached <i>Chef & My Fridge</i>."

109
00:04:28,134 --> 00:04:30,069
They'd ask for the caller,

110
00:04:30,136 --> 00:04:31,904
-and I say, "Kim Sung-ju."
-Yes.

111
00:04:31,971 --> 00:04:33,906
If they go, "Wait, that Kim Sung-ju?"

112
00:04:33,973 --> 00:04:35,608
Then I reply, "Yes, that's me."

113
00:04:36,342 --> 00:04:38,544
Some places then give me
a nice seat by the window.

114
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:40,780
He's like a senior.
He wants better treatment.

115
00:04:40,847 --> 00:04:43,216
When I make a reservation somewhere new,

116
00:04:43,283 --> 00:04:45,018
I do it under someone else's name.

117
00:04:45,084 --> 00:04:47,420
The thing Hyun-suk gets attacked for
has come to me.

118
00:04:47,487 --> 00:04:49,589
He should've said this.

119
00:04:50,757 --> 00:04:53,626
-You need to talk less with age.
-All right.

120
00:04:54,127 --> 00:04:57,363
A TIP FROM THE MOST TALKATIVE FRIEND

121
00:04:57,430 --> 00:04:59,098
Anyway, sorry about that.

122
00:04:59,165 --> 00:05:00,800
Here's the thing.

123
00:05:00,867 --> 00:05:03,870
Let's say you went somewhere
and confirmed that the place is good,

124
00:05:03,936 --> 00:05:06,205
so you now want to befriend the owner.

125
00:05:06,272 --> 00:05:07,473
-That's important.
-I'm sure

126
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:10,009
-you have a secret to becoming friends.
-He must.

127
00:05:10,076 --> 00:05:12,712
I'm quite introverted,

128
00:05:12,779 --> 00:05:14,647
so I can never strike up a conversation.

129
00:05:14,714 --> 00:05:18,851
If I like a place a lot,
I keep going for two to three years.

130
00:05:18,918 --> 00:05:20,086
After some time,

131
00:05:20,153 --> 00:05:22,989
I slowly initiate a conversation

132
00:05:23,056 --> 00:05:25,525
by saying things like,
"Your restaurant looks so clean."

133
00:05:25,591 --> 00:05:27,427
-That takes you two to three years?
-Yes.

134
00:05:27,493 --> 00:05:28,928
-Not on the first day?
-No.

135
00:05:28,995 --> 00:05:30,463
COMPLETE INTROVERT

136
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:32,799
It depends on the personality,
but some people go…

137
00:05:32,865 --> 00:05:34,133
AMAZED

138
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:35,501
"This place is the best."

139
00:05:35,568 --> 00:05:37,804
Who makes that sound
and does this these days?

140
00:05:37,870 --> 00:05:40,640
We're no longer living
in the age of early 2000s infomercials!

141
00:05:40,973 --> 00:05:43,710
BURST OUT IN A LAUGHTER!

142
00:05:43,776 --> 00:05:45,778
Do you clap when you get your food?

143
00:05:45,845 --> 00:05:47,146
KIM SUNG-JU, AGE 55
WOW!

144
00:05:47,213 --> 00:05:50,383
Your food is so good. This is the best!

145
00:05:50,883 --> 00:05:52,452
These days, people leave online reviews.

146
00:05:52,518 --> 00:05:53,486
-They do?
-Yes.

147
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:55,488
MISSING THE OLD THINGS…

148
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:58,391
-Did you become acquainted with a few?
-Yes.

149
00:05:58,458 --> 00:06:01,394
There's a place in Seochon
called A restaurant.

150
00:06:01,461 --> 00:06:02,795
NAM-NO KNOWS THAT PLACE TOO

151
00:06:02,862 --> 00:06:05,865
I've been going there for 15 years,

152
00:06:05,932 --> 00:06:09,068
so the owner there
and I actually became friends.

153
00:06:09,135 --> 00:06:11,104
We've traveled together,

154
00:06:11,170 --> 00:06:13,473
and we know what's going on
with each other's families.

155
00:06:13,539 --> 00:06:15,174
Our mothers are close too.

156
00:06:15,241 --> 00:06:20,646
Can you share a few good places
you've been going to for years?

157
00:06:20,713 --> 00:06:21,914
-Gosh.
-Yes?

158
00:06:21,981 --> 00:06:25,184
I'm not sure
if I can share this on a show.

159
00:06:25,251 --> 00:06:26,285
Or places with good vibes.

160
00:06:26,352 --> 00:06:27,687
-Recently…
-Yes?

161
00:06:29,255 --> 00:06:30,256
Recently?

162
00:06:31,224 --> 00:06:33,593
I went to Yeonsinnae for a shoot.

163
00:06:33,659 --> 00:06:35,061
Yeonsinnae.

164
00:06:35,128 --> 00:06:38,097
There was a place that touched my heart
with an ordinary lunch.

165
00:06:38,164 --> 00:06:39,132
Touched?

166
00:06:39,198 --> 00:06:40,633
It was a Hamheung <i>naengmyeon</i> place.

167
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:42,034
Hamheung-style <i>naengmyeon.</i>

168
00:06:42,101 --> 00:06:43,536
They only had four tables.

169
00:06:43,603 --> 00:06:46,038
-Only four? It must be tiny.
-What? Just four?

170
00:06:46,105 --> 00:06:47,440
Yes, only four tables,

171
00:06:47,507 --> 00:06:49,409
and exactly three items on the menu.

172
00:06:49,475 --> 00:06:51,978
<i>Mul-naengmyeon, bibim-naengmyeon,</i>
<i>hoe-naengmyeon</i>… Wait, one more.

173
00:06:52,044 --> 00:06:53,246
Dumplings. Big-sized <i>mandu</i>.

174
00:06:53,312 --> 00:06:54,514
A must at a <i>naengmyeon</i> place.

175
00:06:54,580 --> 00:06:56,549
So we ordered two <i>naengmyeon</i>
and one <i>mandu</i>,

176
00:06:56,616 --> 00:07:00,319
and most places like these
usually serve ready-made products, right?

177
00:07:00,386 --> 00:07:02,054
Otherwise, it's not cost-effective.

178
00:07:02,121 --> 00:07:03,055
INHALES

179
00:07:03,122 --> 00:07:05,324
When I looked, their two workers

180
00:07:05,391 --> 00:07:08,027
were wearing white uniforms
and white hats.

181
00:07:08,394 --> 00:07:09,762
IMPRESSIVE UNIFORMS

182
00:07:09,829 --> 00:07:11,097
Isn't it odd?

183
00:07:11,164 --> 00:07:12,932
So I sat there and looked,
then noticed them

184
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:14,300
making noodles from scratch.

185
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:15,635
IN THE RESTAURANT?

186
00:07:15,701 --> 00:07:17,470
And the <i>mandu</i> was this big.

187
00:07:17,537 --> 00:07:20,940
So I cut it,
and the veggies inside spilled out.

188
00:07:21,007 --> 00:07:23,976
I asked, "Do you make these here?"

189
00:07:24,043 --> 00:07:25,611
And they said, "Every morning."

190
00:07:25,945 --> 00:07:27,380
EVERYTHING IS HANDMADE

191
00:07:27,447 --> 00:07:28,915
That was such a touching moment.

192
00:07:28,981 --> 00:07:30,883
I'll look up that <i>naengmyeon</i> place.

193
00:07:30,950 --> 00:07:32,051
It's in Yeonsinnae.

194
00:07:32,118 --> 00:07:33,820
-Was it good?
-It was so delicious.

195
00:07:33,886 --> 00:07:35,655
I should eat there too.
The word will go out.

196
00:07:35,721 --> 00:07:37,423
It was the most delicious <i>naengmyeon</i>

197
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:40,293
I have had
in the last three to four years.

198
00:07:40,359 --> 00:07:41,394
I'm interested.

199
00:07:41,461 --> 00:07:42,762
What's the place called?

200
00:07:42,829 --> 00:07:45,598
The name of the place
doesn't sound that good.

201
00:07:45,665 --> 00:07:49,035
-What is it?
-It was N Generation in Yeonsinnae.

202
00:07:49,101 --> 00:07:50,136
-N Generation?
-N Generation?

203
00:07:50,203 --> 00:07:51,704
JOTTING DOWN

204
00:07:51,771 --> 00:07:53,272
-N Generation?
-Not Girls' Generation?

205
00:07:53,339 --> 00:07:56,175
It's like, "Right now, it's N Generation!"

206
00:07:56,242 --> 00:07:59,879
I remember feeling deeply touched
after eating there.

207
00:07:59,946 --> 00:08:01,681
-That's new.
-I see.

208
00:08:01,747 --> 00:08:02,949
One of our chefs

209
00:08:03,015 --> 00:08:05,952
once shared his favorite restaurant

210
00:08:06,018 --> 00:08:08,988
and can no longer even make a visit.

211
00:08:09,822 --> 00:08:11,624
Chef Jong-won?

212
00:08:11,691 --> 00:08:13,025
Did this happen?

213
00:08:13,092 --> 00:08:15,628
During an interview,

214
00:08:15,695 --> 00:08:18,064
I was asked
to share my favorite restaurant.

215
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:20,366
And of course,
you want to share what you like, right?

216
00:08:20,433 --> 00:08:22,101
-Yes.
-There was this bakery I loved,

217
00:08:22,168 --> 00:08:24,036
so I shared it like it wasn't a big deal.

218
00:08:24,103 --> 00:08:26,439
-Okay.
-But now that place has a waiting list.

219
00:08:26,506 --> 00:08:27,874
It blew up.

220
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:30,176
-And there's a limit to what you can get.
-Right.

221
00:08:30,243 --> 00:08:33,179
It became too famous,
so I haven't been able to go either.

222
00:08:33,246 --> 00:08:37,250
You said it in a way
that'd make people want to go.

223
00:08:37,316 --> 00:08:38,150
What did he say?

224
00:08:38,217 --> 00:08:41,220
"I can hear the sound of a trumpet
from the sky when I eat this."

225
00:08:41,787 --> 00:08:45,191
"If I could eat one thing before death,
I'd choose this."

226
00:08:45,258 --> 00:08:49,262
What does it feel like
to hear trumpets in your ears?

227
00:08:49,328 --> 00:08:52,131
Don't angels sound the trumpet
right before your death?

228
00:08:52,565 --> 00:08:55,635
THE TASTE THAT LEADS YOU TO PARADISE

229
00:08:55,701 --> 00:08:58,004
Who wouldn't go after hearing this?

230
00:08:58,070 --> 00:09:01,073
He did the interview like it was
a <i>Chef & My Fridge</i> food review.

231
00:09:01,140 --> 00:09:02,475
Don't you agree?

232
00:09:02,542 --> 00:09:05,745
There's this place we both love
in Hannam-dong.

233
00:09:05,811 --> 00:09:07,380
-<i>Gamja-tang</i>.
-That place.

234
00:09:07,446 --> 00:09:10,349
I used to have team dinners there
when I started 30 years ago,

235
00:09:10,416 --> 00:09:11,984
and it always had a table at any time.

236
00:09:12,051 --> 00:09:15,054
Once it blew up,
people began to line up even at 1 a.m.

237
00:09:15,121 --> 00:09:16,389
Then go at 2 a.m.

238
00:09:16,722 --> 00:09:17,957
PRESENTING A SOLUTION

239
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:20,126
-Go at 2 a.m.
-You can go at 2 a.m.

240
00:09:20,192 --> 00:09:22,061
The attack has returned to you.

241
00:09:22,128 --> 00:09:25,231
You can always go at 2 a.m.
What's the problem?

242
00:09:25,298 --> 00:09:27,199
Right! I could go at 2 a.m.!

243
00:09:27,266 --> 00:09:28,334
Don't line up at 1 a.m.

244
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:30,036
You go to sleep way too early.

245
00:09:30,102 --> 00:09:33,339
OLD ENOUGH TO SLEEP EARLY
AND WAKE UP EARLY…

246
00:09:33,406 --> 00:09:34,941
Dae-myung, let's say

247
00:09:35,007 --> 00:09:37,310
you went somewhere to eat,

248
00:09:37,376 --> 00:09:39,579
but it wasn't as good as you thought.

249
00:09:39,645 --> 00:09:40,947
-Right.
-After he ate there?

250
00:09:41,013 --> 00:09:42,048
-It happens.
-It wasn't good.

251
00:09:42,114 --> 00:09:43,916
-It happens too often.
-You had heard good things.

252
00:09:43,983 --> 00:09:45,384
Then how do you feel?

253
00:09:45,451 --> 00:09:47,053
I just don't have to show it.

254
00:09:47,119 --> 00:09:50,323
But if the owner asks
and insists on getting feedback,

255
00:09:50,389 --> 00:09:54,860
I try my best to find the best part,
even if I didn't like it.

256
00:09:55,127 --> 00:09:56,062
AH-HA

257
00:09:56,128 --> 00:09:59,966
For example, let's say the noodles
were too soggy at a <i>kal-guksu</i> place.

258
00:10:00,032 --> 00:10:04,070
I go, "The food here will
definitely help me digest better."

259
00:10:04,136 --> 00:10:05,771
That's scary.

260
00:10:05,838 --> 00:10:08,407
If the noodles were undercooked,

261
00:10:08,874 --> 00:10:10,576
I say, "There's a bite to it."

262
00:10:10,876 --> 00:10:11,811
VEILED CRITICISM

263
00:10:11,877 --> 00:10:13,346
"I love the texture."

264
00:10:13,412 --> 00:10:14,981
I hope they understand what it means.

265
00:10:15,047 --> 00:10:16,782
-He finds something.
-He's indirect about it.

266
00:10:16,849 --> 00:10:19,919
-Listen closely to his reviews today.
-Yes, chefs.

267
00:10:19,986 --> 00:10:21,487
I see. Between the lines?

268
00:10:21,554 --> 00:10:23,122
Make sure you listen carefully.

269
00:10:23,189 --> 00:10:25,424
The biggest foodie
in the entertainment business,

270
00:10:25,491 --> 00:10:29,695
Dae-myung, brought his fridge,
and I wonder what he has inside.

271
00:10:29,762 --> 00:10:31,664
-Do you use it alone?
-Yes.

272
00:10:31,731 --> 00:10:33,599
I moved out while filming
<i>Misaeng: Incomplete Life,</i>

273
00:10:33,666 --> 00:10:36,302
because I felt bad
about coming home so late.

274
00:10:36,369 --> 00:10:38,938
So it's been a decade, about 12 years.

275
00:10:39,005 --> 00:10:42,274
We may not see too many ingredients
because you're in a production now.

276
00:10:42,341 --> 00:10:44,644
I clean it out when the production begins,

277
00:10:44,710 --> 00:10:47,179
because I end up eating out
most of the time.

278
00:10:47,246 --> 00:10:48,881
But toward the end of it,

279
00:10:48,948 --> 00:10:50,816
I find time to fill it up again.

280
00:10:50,883 --> 00:10:53,519
I keep filling it up and tell myself,
"The filming will end soon."

281
00:10:53,586 --> 00:10:55,187
I fill it with what I want to eat.

282
00:10:55,254 --> 00:10:58,357
I tell myself, "I'll make this
and eat it on the day it's over."

283
00:10:58,424 --> 00:11:00,192
-I see.
-So I keep filling it up.

284
00:11:00,259 --> 00:11:03,562
He's almost done filming the drama,
so I'm sure it's quite full now.

285
00:11:03,629 --> 00:11:05,164
Let's look forward to what we'll find.

286
00:11:05,231 --> 00:11:09,135
He's a foodie, so I'm looking forward
to seeing what he has.

287
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:11,871
Now, we present Dae-myung's fridge!

288
00:11:11,937 --> 00:11:13,472
The foodie's fridge.

289
00:11:13,539 --> 00:11:14,607
THEY'RE AMAZED?

290
00:11:14,674 --> 00:11:16,742
-It's pretty full.
-That's not so bad.

291
00:11:16,809 --> 00:11:18,611
-The foodie's fridge.
-The foodie!

292
00:11:18,678 --> 00:11:20,446
PRESENTING DAE-MYUNG'S FRIDGE

293
00:11:22,148 --> 00:11:23,182
COMPLETELY FULL

294
00:11:23,249 --> 00:11:26,452
Look at this. He has all these sauces.

295
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:28,587
WHOA…

296
00:11:28,654 --> 00:11:30,456
On the very top shelf,

297
00:11:30,523 --> 00:11:32,291
there's one lone bread.

298
00:11:32,358 --> 00:11:34,427
-Is this for when you feel peckish?
-That's…

299
00:11:34,493 --> 00:11:37,163
-That's a really good one.
-The fact that it's in the fridge means

300
00:11:37,229 --> 00:11:38,531
I drank the night before.

301
00:11:39,632 --> 00:11:40,966
-Is that his hangover cure?
-Carbs.

302
00:11:41,033 --> 00:11:42,802
Some people buy stuff when they're drunk.

303
00:11:42,868 --> 00:11:45,838
On my way home
after drinking outside, for some reason,

304
00:11:45,905 --> 00:11:47,973
I always stop at a convenience store.

305
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:49,275
A DRUNK PURCHASE

306
00:11:49,341 --> 00:11:50,710
I always need that sweet bun.

307
00:11:50,776 --> 00:11:52,478
So it wasn't just me.

308
00:11:52,545 --> 00:11:55,281
I'm not sure if you know,
but that bread is great with <i>makgeolli</i>.

309
00:11:55,347 --> 00:11:56,348
OH, MY

310
00:11:56,415 --> 00:11:57,249
You're right.

311
00:11:57,316 --> 00:12:00,486
Try a bite of that sweet bun
with <i>makgeolli</i>, rice wine.

312
00:12:00,553 --> 00:12:02,188
You won't even remember where you are.

313
00:12:02,254 --> 00:12:04,323
It goes really well with that rice aroma.

314
00:12:04,390 --> 00:12:06,492
-Really? I didn't know that.
-Really?

315
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:08,194
Also with <i>cheongju</i>, refined rice wine.

316
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:10,429
-Why are you so excited?
-I'm serious.

317
00:12:10,496 --> 00:12:12,665
-You're a lot like me.
-He's excited.

318
00:12:12,732 --> 00:12:14,200
-He is.
-They're a good match.

319
00:12:14,266 --> 00:12:18,070
Is this something you got delivered?

320
00:12:18,137 --> 00:12:20,439
It's from my favorite <i>bugeo-guk</i> place
in Mugyo-dong.

321
00:12:20,506 --> 00:12:21,807
In Hannam-dong?

322
00:12:21,874 --> 00:12:23,442
-No, Mugyo-dong.
-Mugyo-dong.

323
00:12:23,509 --> 00:12:25,244
Calm down! Keep your cool!

324
00:12:25,311 --> 00:12:27,379
My mouth is suddenly watering.

325
00:12:27,446 --> 00:12:31,951
I recently had a shoot nearby,
so I got it to go for after the shoot.

326
00:12:32,017 --> 00:12:34,487
He kept it on stand-by.

327
00:12:34,553 --> 00:12:35,855
Why do you like this place?

328
00:12:35,921 --> 00:12:37,323
As a starter,

329
00:12:37,389 --> 00:12:41,660
you get <i>saeu-jeot</i>, salted shrimp,
mixed with green onion and sesame oil.

330
00:12:41,727 --> 00:12:44,029
-Salted shrimp?
-Yes, <i>saeu-jeot.</i>

331
00:12:44,096 --> 00:12:45,498
And I always order a fried egg.

332
00:12:45,564 --> 00:12:46,732
I love fried eggs.

333
00:12:46,799 --> 00:12:49,802
I mix everything with my rice
before the soup is out

334
00:12:49,869 --> 00:12:53,539
-and have a bite with closed eyes.
-He has a routine.

335
00:12:53,606 --> 00:12:55,207
Then I'm already in paradise.

336
00:12:55,508 --> 00:12:57,376
I KNOW WHAT THOSE TRUMPETS SOUND LIKE

337
00:12:57,443 --> 00:12:58,811
He's my type.

338
00:12:58,878 --> 00:13:01,781
Once I have a sip of the soup
with that rice, it's over.

339
00:13:01,847 --> 00:13:04,717
-It must be that good.
-I wouldn't regret it even if I died.

340
00:13:04,784 --> 00:13:07,319
-I want to swap seats with Jong-won.
-What else did you buy?

341
00:13:07,386 --> 00:13:09,555
-Some herring roe <i>jeotgal</i>.
-This one?

342
00:13:09,622 --> 00:13:11,690
It's from my friend's place, A restaurant.

343
00:13:11,757 --> 00:13:12,591
Herring roe <i>jeotgal</i>.

344
00:13:12,658 --> 00:13:15,294
-Herring roe.
-It's great when you have no appetite.

345
00:13:15,361 --> 00:13:18,297
-That and some rice.
-You just need a spoon of that on rice

346
00:13:18,364 --> 00:13:20,366
with a dash of sesame oil.

347
00:13:20,432 --> 00:13:22,368
-Roasted seaweed.
-And roasted seaweed.

348
00:13:22,434 --> 00:13:23,836
Or with <i>gamtae</i>, lattice-like seaweed.

349
00:13:23,903 --> 00:13:25,604
-It's amazing.
-I have <i>gamtae </i>in my fridge!

350
00:13:25,671 --> 00:13:29,074
-I knew it! <i>Gamtae</i>! You're a foodie!
-It's true.

351
00:13:29,141 --> 00:13:29,975
I have <i>gamtae.</i>

352
00:13:30,242 --> 00:13:32,444
IT'S THAT GOOD?

353
00:13:32,511 --> 00:13:34,380
-Herring roe <i>jeotgal </i>with tofu.
-He has it.

354
00:13:34,446 --> 00:13:37,316
I like to eat it with some tofu
and cucumber,

355
00:13:37,383 --> 00:13:38,617
wrapped in <i>gamtae</i>.

356
00:13:38,884 --> 00:13:39,919
ENGAGED

357
00:13:39,985 --> 00:13:40,820
Then I end up where?

358
00:13:40,886 --> 00:13:42,488
-Paradise.
-Paradise.

359
00:13:42,555 --> 00:13:44,456
He became eloquent
the moment we opened his fridge.

360
00:13:44,523 --> 00:13:46,125
I FOUND MY MAN

361
00:13:46,192 --> 00:13:49,195
He's so passionate about food,
much more than his acting career.

362
00:13:50,129 --> 00:13:51,397
I'm so happy right now.

363
00:13:51,463 --> 00:13:53,232
-What's this broth?
-That?

364
00:13:53,299 --> 00:13:54,900
It's <i>naengmyeon</i> broth.

365
00:13:54,967 --> 00:13:56,502
<i>Naengmyeon</i> broth.

366
00:13:56,569 --> 00:13:59,104
-There's a DJ friend of mine, Tiger Disco.
-A DJ?

367
00:13:59,171 --> 00:14:00,639
-Wait, Tiger Disco?
-Yes.

368
00:14:00,706 --> 00:14:03,709
He runs a bar at night,
playing his own music.

369
00:14:03,776 --> 00:14:07,112
But in the daytime, he opens it
for three hours as a <i>naengmyeon</i> place.

370
00:14:07,179 --> 00:14:08,714
A three-hour <i>naengmyeon </i>place?

371
00:14:08,781 --> 00:14:11,317
-He's a cook and a DJ?
-He's originally a cook.

372
00:14:11,517 --> 00:14:13,385
HOW INTERESTING

373
00:14:13,452 --> 00:14:15,020
He only makes <i>naengmyeon</i> in summer,

374
00:14:15,087 --> 00:14:16,755
but since I can't go because of shoots,

375
00:14:16,822 --> 00:14:18,224
he gives me just the broth.

376
00:14:18,290 --> 00:14:22,127
When I want it but can't go out for it,
I cook some buckwheat noodles

377
00:14:22,194 --> 00:14:24,396
and eat it with the broth.
And where do I end up?

378
00:14:24,463 --> 00:14:26,932
-Paradise.
-Paradise.

379
00:14:27,132 --> 00:14:29,168
AMAZED

380
00:14:29,235 --> 00:14:32,271
-It's his testimony.
-So you have a place for everything.

381
00:14:32,338 --> 00:14:34,106
Which style do you prefer?
Hamheung or Pyongyang?

382
00:14:34,173 --> 00:14:36,075
-That's Pyongyang-style.
-Pyongyang <i>naengmyeon</i>.

383
00:14:36,141 --> 00:14:37,676
Is there a reason why you like it?

384
00:14:37,743 --> 00:14:40,045
Pyongyang <i>naengmyeon </i>feels like

385
00:14:40,112 --> 00:14:42,948
someone I had loved for a long time,
my first love.

386
00:14:43,015 --> 00:14:44,817
-Right.
-Your first love?

387
00:14:44,884 --> 00:14:47,386
I'd forget about it,
but it'd suddenly hit me again.

388
00:14:47,453 --> 00:14:49,889
-Suddenly.
-And a particular season reminds me of it.

389
00:14:49,955 --> 00:14:52,658
I recommend that
you try it on a snowy winter day.

390
00:14:52,725 --> 00:14:56,595
Try eating <i>Pyongyang</i> naengmyeon
by the window as it snows.

391
00:14:56,662 --> 00:14:59,698
-Have a bite as you look at the snow…
-How romantic.

392
00:14:59,765 --> 00:15:02,334
-…with a glass of soju.
-He knows what's up.

393
00:15:02,401 --> 00:15:04,203
-Recommend a place for us.
-Actually.

394
00:15:04,270 --> 00:15:07,606
I like P restaurant, J restaurant,

395
00:15:07,673 --> 00:15:09,942
and even U restaurant in Uijeongbu.

396
00:15:10,009 --> 00:15:13,979
I don't get to go there often,
so I like to go to the place in Uijeongbu.

397
00:15:14,747 --> 00:15:17,449
There's a certain excitement
while I'm on my way there.

398
00:15:17,516 --> 00:15:19,018
-The anticipation?
-Yes.

399
00:15:19,084 --> 00:15:21,387
-It's part of his dining experience.
-He's already dining.

400
00:15:21,453 --> 00:15:22,888
The trip there is the appetizer.

401
00:15:22,955 --> 00:15:25,424
On my way there, I imagine
ordering cold boiled pork in my mind.

402
00:15:25,491 --> 00:15:26,759
-Gosh.
-Cold boiled pork.

403
00:15:26,825 --> 00:15:28,494
And even mung bean <i>jeon.</i>

404
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:31,697
I run through everything in my head,
so it's exciting to see it in person.

405
00:15:32,798 --> 00:15:34,199
-And if it snows…
-He's a romantic.

406
00:15:34,266 --> 00:15:36,635
He wasn't like this
when he talked about his acting career.

407
00:15:36,702 --> 00:15:39,672
I never knew
he was so good with his words.

408
00:15:39,738 --> 00:15:42,641
His eyes are so sparkly right now.

409
00:15:42,708 --> 00:15:45,511
I see some cheese down here.

410
00:15:45,577 --> 00:15:49,114
-I like white wine in summer.
-You have a few.

411
00:15:49,181 --> 00:15:53,686
I even purchase
boxes of the same white wine for myself.

412
00:15:53,752 --> 00:15:55,688
-You get these in bulk?
-Yes.

413
00:15:55,754 --> 00:15:58,390
-He buys it by the box.
-It's true.

414
00:15:58,457 --> 00:16:00,960
Tell us the snacks
that are good with white wine.

415
00:16:01,026 --> 00:16:04,029
I bought some Korean melons to eat
after the shoots are over.

416
00:16:04,096 --> 00:16:08,100
<i>I like to eat them</i>
<i>with mascarpone and olive oil.</i>

417
00:16:08,167 --> 00:16:10,002
I SEE

418
00:16:10,069 --> 00:16:11,837
-It's really good.
-I think

419
00:16:11,904 --> 00:16:14,573
-it'll taste a bit like vanilla.
-Right.

420
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:17,676
-Do you know what's good with white wine?
-What?

421
00:16:17,743 --> 00:16:18,877
Red wine.

422
00:16:19,144 --> 00:16:21,580
ON A WHOLE DIFFERENT LEVEL

423
00:16:21,647 --> 00:16:24,483
Drink lots of white wine with sips of red.

424
00:16:24,783 --> 00:16:26,118
DEEPLY MOVED

425
00:16:26,185 --> 00:16:27,853
It's delicious.

426
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:29,555
IT'S DELICIOUS, I TELL YOU

427
00:16:29,621 --> 00:16:31,557
You're creating rosé wine in your stomach.

428
00:16:31,623 --> 00:16:33,692
-You have lots of veggies.
-Vegetables.

429
00:16:33,759 --> 00:16:36,161
-Cilantro. Dill.
-Herbs.

430
00:16:36,228 --> 00:16:37,496
These are all herbs. Parsley.

431
00:16:37,563 --> 00:16:41,600
One of the things I'm going to make
after the shoots is tacos.

432
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:43,702
-I plan to make tacos with those.
-Tacos.

433
00:16:43,769 --> 00:16:46,939
So cilantro is for those,
and dill is for tzatziki.

434
00:16:47,006 --> 00:16:48,874
-Do you enjoy Mexican food?
-I do.

435
00:16:49,141 --> 00:16:50,976
HE LOVES MEXICAN FOOD!

436
00:16:51,043 --> 00:16:54,947
And as for the cilantro,
I recently tried cilantro rice.

437
00:16:55,014 --> 00:16:57,983
<i>It was probably cream sauce,</i>
<i>seafood, and cilantro</i>

438
00:16:58,050 --> 00:17:00,586
with long-grain rice. It was really good.

439
00:17:00,652 --> 00:17:02,554
-You have long-grain rice?
-I do.

440
00:17:02,621 --> 00:17:04,423
-You have long-grain rice?
-Long-grain rice?

441
00:17:04,490 --> 00:17:07,026
-There it is.
-I keep jasmine rice around.

442
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:09,595
Are you currently hooked on Mexican food?

443
00:17:09,661 --> 00:17:11,063
Yes, I love it.

444
00:17:11,130 --> 00:17:12,998
Is that Patrón in the freezer?

445
00:17:13,065 --> 00:17:14,233
Yes! It's Patrón silver.

446
00:17:14,299 --> 00:17:16,568
-Is this tequila?
-Yes.

447
00:17:16,635 --> 00:17:18,470
-I got that for tacos.
-And it's in the freezer.

448
00:17:19,204 --> 00:17:22,141
At this rate,
you might as well visit Mexico.

449
00:17:22,207 --> 00:17:23,675
I really want to.

450
00:17:23,742 --> 00:17:25,811
-I want to go see a World Cup game there,
-I see.

451
00:17:25,878 --> 00:17:27,413
but my schedule wouldn't let me.

452
00:17:28,213 --> 00:17:30,449
-You have to shoot the drama.
-Next to the tequila,

453
00:17:30,516 --> 00:17:32,051
I kept some orange juice frozen.

454
00:17:32,518 --> 00:17:34,286
WHAT ARE THESE FOR?

455
00:17:34,353 --> 00:17:36,522
-You'll mix it with tequila.
-Yes!

456
00:17:36,588 --> 00:17:37,990
-Instead of ice.
-It's so easy.

457
00:17:38,223 --> 00:17:40,159
GOES ALL IN EVEN FOR A DRINK

458
00:17:40,225 --> 00:17:41,527
I want to hang out at your place.

459
00:17:41,593 --> 00:17:43,195
Come over sometime.

460
00:17:43,262 --> 00:17:45,631
-You might end up there today.
-I want to go today.

461
00:17:45,697 --> 00:17:49,034
-Grilled lamb skewer sauce?
-Lamb skewer.

462
00:17:49,101 --> 00:17:52,938
You can use that seasoning
whenever you cook meat,

463
00:17:53,005 --> 00:17:54,239
and it's good with <i>ramyeon</i> too.

464
00:17:54,506 --> 00:17:55,374
THAT'S IT

465
00:17:55,441 --> 00:17:57,109
Most people don't know that.

466
00:17:57,176 --> 00:17:59,211
It turns <i>ramyeon</i>
into <i>gamja-tang</i>, pork backbone stew.

467
00:17:59,545 --> 00:18:01,146
NAM-NO FALLS IN LOVE

468
00:18:01,213 --> 00:18:03,615
Most people have no idea
that they go well together.

469
00:18:03,682 --> 00:18:05,818
Pour instant curry
over frozen udon noodles,

470
00:18:05,884 --> 00:18:08,020
sprinkle that, and microwave.

471
00:18:08,087 --> 00:18:11,123
-It becomes restaurant-grade curry udon.
-Yes, exactly.

472
00:18:11,190 --> 00:18:14,893
-I have garam masala next to that.
-Garam masala.

473
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:18,230
The moment you throw that into curry,
you're immediately in India.

474
00:18:18,297 --> 00:18:19,398
You're right.

475
00:18:19,465 --> 00:18:21,300
This episode is
so different from the last one.

476
00:18:21,366 --> 00:18:25,871
There's a passionate debate going on,
but Min, you can't even take part.

477
00:18:25,938 --> 00:18:29,508
Yes, I mostly order delivery food at home.

478
00:18:29,575 --> 00:18:32,578
-I mean, that's amazing.
-You probably didn't know any of this.

479
00:18:32,644 --> 00:18:34,680
Look at what he did with the green onion.

480
00:18:34,746 --> 00:18:36,882
-Yes, all these.
-And the bread too.

481
00:18:36,949 --> 00:18:39,051
He even portioned these noodles.

482
00:18:39,118 --> 00:18:40,552
Noodles? <i>Kal-guksu</i>?

483
00:18:40,619 --> 00:18:41,553
Yes, <i>kal-guksu</i> noodles.

484
00:18:42,020 --> 00:18:43,689
EMBARRASSED

485
00:18:43,889 --> 00:18:45,424
I sound way too excited.

486
00:18:45,491 --> 00:18:48,727
My favorite <i>kal-guksu</i> place
is in Hongeun-dong.

487
00:18:48,794 --> 00:18:51,096
And when I get the food to go,
they always give extra noodles,

488
00:18:51,163 --> 00:18:53,732
so I always have leftovers.

489
00:18:53,799 --> 00:18:55,567
I keep them frozen,

490
00:18:55,634 --> 00:18:58,837
and when I make <i>ramyeon</i>,
I add those instead of <i>ramyeon</i> noodles.

491
00:18:58,904 --> 00:19:01,006
-It makes the soup thicker.
-And not <i>ramyeon </i>noodles?

492
00:19:01,073 --> 00:19:03,809
So you got everything you want to eat
ready to go right after the wrap.

493
00:19:03,876 --> 00:19:05,944
-They're all waiting for me.
-They're waiting.

494
00:19:06,011 --> 00:19:08,013
I wouldn't call that a fridge.

495
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:10,015
It's a curated boutique.

496
00:19:10,082 --> 00:19:12,284
-It's a food boutique.
-It is a boutique.

497
00:19:12,351 --> 00:19:14,553
The buyer has selected the items

498
00:19:14,620 --> 00:19:16,455
to adorn the fridge with.

499
00:19:16,522 --> 00:19:19,091
-All selected.
-Yes, every single one.

500
00:19:19,158 --> 00:19:20,559
-Let's say it wrapped.
-So amazing.

501
00:19:20,626 --> 00:19:21,760
I'll open it

502
00:19:21,827 --> 00:19:24,429
and chug a can of beer immediately.

503
00:19:24,696 --> 00:19:26,298
ALSO INVESTED

504
00:19:26,365 --> 00:19:28,467
And then I'll make <i>ramyeon</i>.

505
00:19:29,434 --> 00:19:31,103
-With those noodles?
-Those noodles.

506
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:33,906
And as I make the noodles,
I'll throw in thin pork belly.

507
00:19:34,206 --> 00:19:35,374
YOU GET 100 POINTS OUT OF 100

508
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:36,675
I also have minced garlic,

509
00:19:36,742 --> 00:19:38,744
-so a spoonful of that too.
-You need some garlic.

510
00:19:38,810 --> 00:19:40,279
You need garlic in <i>ramyeon</i>.

511
00:19:40,345 --> 00:19:42,080
Do you enjoy red meat?

512
00:19:42,147 --> 00:19:44,316
-I do.
-He has lamb.

513
00:19:44,383 --> 00:19:46,118
I bought the lamb

514
00:19:46,185 --> 00:19:48,754
to eat as one of the fillings
when I make tacos.

515
00:19:48,820 --> 00:19:50,155
Did you get these somewhere?

516
00:19:50,222 --> 00:19:51,723
-They don't seem store-bought.
-No.

517
00:19:51,790 --> 00:19:54,893
The way they're packed
doesn't seem like it's from a supermarket.

518
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:57,563
There's a butcher shop
I frequent in Yaksu-dong.

519
00:19:57,796 --> 00:19:59,364
HE KNOWS GOOD PLACES EVERYWHERE

520
00:19:59,431 --> 00:20:00,399
-All of Korea.
-Yaksu-dong.

521
00:20:00,465 --> 00:20:04,937
I live alone, so I can't eat
a huge lump of meat all at once.

522
00:20:05,003 --> 00:20:08,473
So when I ask the owner,
I can get them packed in single servings.

523
00:20:08,540 --> 00:20:11,310
As you already know,
it doesn't taste the same

524
00:20:11,376 --> 00:20:12,444
once the meat is frozen.

525
00:20:12,511 --> 00:20:14,346
KIM DAE-MYUNG'S SOUL MATE

526
00:20:14,413 --> 00:20:17,983
But vacuum-sealed meat will last

527
00:20:18,050 --> 00:20:20,786
up to three weeks in the fridge.

528
00:20:20,852 --> 00:20:24,656
-They're also waiting for the wrap.
-They are.

529
00:20:24,723 --> 00:20:26,758
WHY DON'T YOU KNOW THAT I'M WAITING?

530
00:20:26,825 --> 00:20:28,760
Hang in there just a little more.

531
00:20:28,827 --> 00:20:30,829
-He looks so happy.
-Way too happy.

532
00:20:30,896 --> 00:20:33,065
We had to get him to talk
when it came to his career.

533
00:20:33,365 --> 00:20:35,234
THEY DON'T EVEN HAVE TO ASK A QUESTION NOW

534
00:20:35,300 --> 00:20:37,302
We don't even have to ask him questions.

535
00:20:37,369 --> 00:20:41,607
I'm thinking that he might be happier
if he steered his career to this path.

536
00:20:41,673 --> 00:20:45,444
That's all for Dae-myung's fridge,
full of curated flavors!

537
00:20:45,844 --> 00:20:49,047
STILL NOT OVER IT

538
00:20:49,114 --> 00:20:52,317
He's all about the details
and is very serious about food.

539
00:20:52,384 --> 00:20:54,519
I wonder what he wants to eat today.

540
00:20:54,586 --> 00:20:56,488
Let's find out his orders first.

541
00:20:56,555 --> 00:20:58,123
Show us his order!

542
00:20:58,690 --> 00:21:01,193
That's a Wrap, So Head Out!

543
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:03,629
-How romantic.
-As you wait for the wrap,

544
00:21:03,695 --> 00:21:05,330
what exactly are you looking for?

545
00:21:05,397 --> 00:21:09,301
I like to go on a trip
whenever I'm done with a production,

546
00:21:09,368 --> 00:21:12,437
but I don't think
I'll be able to do that this time.

547
00:21:12,504 --> 00:21:16,808
Instead, if you recommend a dish
from a particular country

548
00:21:16,875 --> 00:21:20,479
that I may want to visit, I'll eat that

549
00:21:20,545 --> 00:21:22,914
and imagine that I'm there.

550
00:21:22,981 --> 00:21:25,951
-Living vicariously through food?
-Yes.

551
00:21:26,018 --> 00:21:28,120
-Something that tastes authentic.
-Yes.

552
00:21:28,186 --> 00:21:30,622
A dish from a particular region.

553
00:21:30,689 --> 00:21:32,891
The next one. What's the second order?

554
00:21:33,692 --> 00:21:37,062
-Food, Vibe, Chefs.
-Chefs.

555
00:21:37,129 --> 00:21:40,165
-Not Dae-myung, but chefs?
-Yes.

556
00:21:40,232 --> 00:21:44,369
I love listening to the stories
chefs have to tell,

557
00:21:44,436 --> 00:21:46,972
because eating their food
and listening to the stories

558
00:21:47,039 --> 00:21:48,874
feel like a whole story.

559
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:50,909
I wanted to make that order

560
00:21:50,976 --> 00:21:55,480
because I wanted to listen
to the stories you have.

561
00:21:55,547 --> 00:21:58,684
-You're looking for a dish with a story.
-From their memories.

562
00:21:58,750 --> 00:22:01,486
I can think of just one person
who can unravel a story

563
00:22:01,553 --> 00:22:02,954
-like this one.
-Who?

564
00:22:03,021 --> 00:22:03,855
The grandpa.

565
00:22:03,922 --> 00:22:06,091
-He has a lot of stories to tell.
-He does.

566
00:22:06,158 --> 00:22:07,492
-He's full of stories.
-He is.

567
00:22:07,559 --> 00:22:08,660
He has lots of them.

568
00:22:08,727 --> 00:22:09,795
Grandpa's story time.

569
00:22:09,861 --> 00:22:12,230
The culinary path I have taken to this day

570
00:22:12,297 --> 00:22:14,966
shows the philosophy I have as a chef.

571
00:22:15,233 --> 00:22:16,935
MOVED

572
00:22:17,002 --> 00:22:19,137
Of course, I'll go with Food, Vibe, Chefs.

573
00:22:19,204 --> 00:22:21,406
I always convey a message

574
00:22:21,473 --> 00:22:23,075
-whenever I cook.
-Sure.

575
00:22:23,141 --> 00:22:25,277
-You do?
-I always include my philosophy…

576
00:22:25,344 --> 00:22:27,746
-There was a message?
-…to let my guests enjoy it fully.

577
00:22:27,813 --> 00:22:30,482
Today, I will take my story,
the book of my culinary journey,

578
00:22:30,549 --> 00:22:31,883
and read it to you.

579
00:22:33,251 --> 00:22:34,086
His life.

580
00:22:34,152 --> 00:22:36,054
Grandpa's food story time.

581
00:22:36,121 --> 00:22:37,389
YOU PUNK

582
00:22:37,456 --> 00:22:41,193
Chef Hyun-suk is the first one
to take a spot.

583
00:22:41,259 --> 00:22:42,828
Do you want to go next, Ho-young?

584
00:22:42,894 --> 00:22:44,463
Sure. I'll let you

585
00:22:44,529 --> 00:22:46,932
travel to Japan.

586
00:22:47,132 --> 00:22:48,700
THANK YOU

587
00:22:48,767 --> 00:22:51,336
He studied in Osaka.

588
00:22:51,403 --> 00:22:53,672
Ho-young takes a spot at the top.

589
00:22:53,739 --> 00:22:56,141
Only two chefs remain now.

590
00:22:56,208 --> 00:22:57,576
-So which one?
-Jong-won.

591
00:22:57,642 --> 00:22:59,044
You have many second hometowns.

592
00:22:59,111 --> 00:23:01,546
Yes, he's a guy who has lots of hometowns.

593
00:23:01,613 --> 00:23:03,281
EXCHANGING HOMETOWNS

594
00:23:03,348 --> 00:23:04,916
I'll join the first round

595
00:23:04,983 --> 00:23:07,319
and send him to a nice place.

596
00:23:07,619 --> 00:23:09,554
TO A PLACE WHERE TRUMPETS SOUND?

597
00:23:09,621 --> 00:23:11,423
He takes the first round.

598
00:23:11,490 --> 00:23:13,959
-Sam's spot is automatically decided.
-It's automatic.

599
00:23:14,025 --> 00:23:15,494
-Do you have a story to tell?
-A story?

600
00:23:15,560 --> 00:23:17,062
-Me?
-A story.

601
00:23:17,129 --> 00:23:18,997
Yes, there is a story I want to share.

602
00:23:19,064 --> 00:23:21,066
After all,
you've starred in a music video.

603
00:23:21,133 --> 00:23:23,135
Yes, he even acted.

604
00:23:23,335 --> 00:23:25,036
HE HAS LOTS OF STORIES TO TELL

605
00:23:25,103 --> 00:23:27,906
First, That's a Wrap, So Head Out!

606
00:23:27,973 --> 00:23:31,009
Let's begin the round
between Ho-young and Jong-won!

607
00:23:31,076 --> 00:23:33,845
TIME TO LEAVE FOR A CULINARY TRIP

608
00:23:33,912 --> 00:23:37,949
<i>This is the first round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with Dae-myung's ingredients.</i>

609
00:23:38,016 --> 00:23:40,552
Chef Son Jong-won,
who dreams of putting together

610
00:23:40,619 --> 00:23:42,587
the Top Chef's Playlist,

611
00:23:42,654 --> 00:23:45,490
versus Chef Jung Ho-young,
who wants to beat the top chef

612
00:23:45,557 --> 00:23:47,726
and finish with the biggest twist.

613
00:23:47,793 --> 00:23:50,195
Jong-won is
currently the sole first place,

614
00:23:50,262 --> 00:23:51,797
and if he wins today,

615
00:23:51,863 --> 00:23:53,965
he will be the first chef

616
00:23:54,032 --> 00:23:56,067
to have won ten rounds.

617
00:23:56,134 --> 00:23:57,402
-Already?
-Yes.

618
00:23:57,469 --> 00:24:00,272
Ho-young, with five stars,

619
00:24:00,338 --> 00:24:03,542
-was tied with Hyun-suk and Sung-jun.
-You're right.

620
00:24:03,608 --> 00:24:05,243
The Ugliness.

621
00:24:05,477 --> 00:24:06,878
KWON SUNG-JUN WINS

622
00:24:06,945 --> 00:24:08,013
Sung-jun wins!

623
00:24:08,079 --> 00:24:10,248
<i>But Sung-jun won last week,</i>

624
00:24:10,315 --> 00:24:13,518
pushing both Ho-young and Hyun-suk back.

625
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:16,488
That star is a very precious one.

626
00:24:17,055 --> 00:24:19,357
He gave up his looks for it.

627
00:24:21,092 --> 00:24:22,160
SO FUNNY♥

628
00:24:22,227 --> 00:24:23,895
Because I'm a chef.

629
00:24:23,962 --> 00:24:25,730
He's like, "Because I'm a chef."

630
00:24:25,797 --> 00:24:30,569
In the green room, we recorded
a very competitive conversation.

631
00:24:30,635 --> 00:24:33,638
Ho-young said the chef
who has become the most evil

632
00:24:33,705 --> 00:24:36,675
isn't actually Sam, but Jong-won.

633
00:24:36,741 --> 00:24:38,009
ME?

634
00:24:38,076 --> 00:24:39,644
His hometown changes every time,

635
00:24:39,711 --> 00:24:43,114
-Right.
-…and says the things he never did before.

636
00:24:43,181 --> 00:24:44,716
San Francisco and Hong Kong.

637
00:24:44,783 --> 00:24:46,685
I'm quite afraid of him now.

638
00:24:46,751 --> 00:24:47,953
-I see.
-Right.

639
00:24:48,019 --> 00:24:51,423
-He might even start dancing very soon.
-Right?

640
00:24:51,490 --> 00:24:53,058
I heard you came prepared.

641
00:24:53,124 --> 00:24:54,159
A dance?

642
00:24:55,260 --> 00:24:56,094
No?

643
00:24:56,161 --> 00:24:58,196
Well, actually,

644
00:24:58,263 --> 00:25:00,198
I can't say I haven't considered it.

645
00:25:00,398 --> 00:25:01,366
HE HAS CONSIDERED DANCING

646
00:25:01,433 --> 00:25:05,237
-He'll do it someday. Someday.
-It'll happen soon.

647
00:25:05,303 --> 00:25:07,372
-He must've tried dancing at home.
-Actually,

648
00:25:07,439 --> 00:25:09,207
movements are important
when dancing, right?

649
00:25:09,641 --> 00:25:10,942
SNORT

650
00:25:11,009 --> 00:25:12,344
So between JYP Entertainment,

651
00:25:12,410 --> 00:25:13,578
SM Entertainment,

652
00:25:13,645 --> 00:25:15,547
and YG Entertainment,

653
00:25:15,614 --> 00:25:17,516
I haven't chosen which one to train with.

654
00:25:18,016 --> 00:25:18,850
HE'LL PERFECT HIS DANCE

655
00:25:18,917 --> 00:25:20,151
He's a perfectionist.

656
00:25:20,218 --> 00:25:23,688
That means he'll come and dance
the moment he has his moves.

657
00:25:23,755 --> 00:25:25,790
Even Ho-young doesn't get lessons.

658
00:25:25,857 --> 00:25:29,060
I recently got a lesson
from choreographer Bae Yoon-jung herself.

659
00:25:29,127 --> 00:25:30,695
You learned from her?

660
00:25:30,762 --> 00:25:32,497
She told me not to tell
that she taught me.

661
00:25:32,564 --> 00:25:33,999
DON'T TELL ANYONE I TAUGHT YOU

662
00:25:34,933 --> 00:25:36,701
IT'S HARD TO BELIEVE THAT HE LEARNED THESE

663
00:25:36,768 --> 00:25:38,837
His teacher was embarrassed by him.

664
00:25:39,137 --> 00:25:40,972
THE STUDENT REJECTED BY HIS TEACHER

665
00:25:41,039 --> 00:25:42,707
I also hear

666
00:25:42,774 --> 00:25:45,810
Jong-won has been trembling in fear
in preparation for this round.

667
00:25:45,877 --> 00:25:47,412
What's up?

668
00:25:47,479 --> 00:25:48,513
Ho-young seems to have

669
00:25:48,580 --> 00:25:50,749
-an extra cooking tool in the studio.
-Right.

670
00:25:50,815 --> 00:25:52,784
-The human tool.
-Something new?

671
00:25:52,851 --> 00:25:54,920
-It's a one-on-two fight.
-I am also

672
00:25:54,986 --> 00:25:59,925
known as the peddler of the show
because I bring so many things,

673
00:25:59,991 --> 00:26:01,560
but he always uses

674
00:26:01,626 --> 00:26:03,795
a huge-sized tool
that can't even compare to mine.

675
00:26:03,862 --> 00:26:05,063
A HUGE KITCHEN TOOL?

676
00:26:05,697 --> 00:26:07,432
SOMEBODY KIM

677
00:26:07,499 --> 00:26:09,801
-You're sitting in front of him again?
-Ho-young.

678
00:26:09,868 --> 00:26:11,236
It just happened.

679
00:26:11,303 --> 00:26:13,138
So if it's all right with him,

680
00:26:13,204 --> 00:26:15,206
I'd like to add another tool too.

681
00:26:15,273 --> 00:26:17,342
-Is it a person?
-It is.

682
00:26:17,409 --> 00:26:21,046
-So it's a kitchen toolman.
-A kitchen toolman.

683
00:26:21,112 --> 00:26:23,481
Not the kitchen staff, but a toolman.

684
00:26:23,548 --> 00:26:25,183
-At least he calls it human.
-Kitchen toolman.

685
00:26:25,250 --> 00:26:27,986
Ho-young uses Sam, so who will you use?

686
00:26:28,053 --> 00:26:29,254
Nam-no, help me!

687
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:30,155
Yes, sir!

688
00:26:31,957 --> 00:26:35,360
-They'll either both use one or none.
-It's practically a team round.

689
00:26:35,427 --> 00:26:38,997
-I think I'll need him for something.
-So you do need Sam.

690
00:26:39,931 --> 00:26:41,933
CHEF HO-YOUNG'S INGREDIENTS:
LAMB, SHRIMP, NOODLES, CHERRY TOMATO

691
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,002
CILANTRO, MINI BELL PEPPER, LETTUCE,
CURRY BLOCK, GARAM MASALA, AND MORE

692
00:26:44,069 --> 00:26:46,972
What are you making, Chef Ho-young?

693
00:26:47,038 --> 00:26:49,975
The name of my dish is

694
00:26:50,041 --> 00:26:51,142
BOOMBALAMB!

695
00:26:51,209 --> 00:26:52,344
He tries too hard.

696
00:26:52,410 --> 00:26:53,979
Both guests are in shock.

697
00:26:54,045 --> 00:26:55,680
-BLACKPINK?
-What was that?

698
00:26:55,747 --> 00:26:57,716
"BOOMBAYAH" by BLACKPINK.

699
00:26:57,782 --> 00:27:00,585
-I'll make a lamb dish.
-BOOMBALAMB.

700
00:27:00,652 --> 00:27:02,320
Dae-myung said…

701
00:27:02,387 --> 00:27:04,289
-If you're dizzy, take a break.
-Okay.

702
00:27:05,290 --> 00:27:07,392
-You look dizzy.
-The blood rushed to his head.

703
00:27:07,459 --> 00:27:08,994
I want to make

704
00:27:09,060 --> 00:27:11,563
a lamb dish to send Dae-myung

705
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:13,298
to Sapporo.

706
00:27:13,365 --> 00:27:15,066
In July,

707
00:27:15,133 --> 00:27:18,169
-the weather is cool and nice there.
-It's better in summer.

708
00:27:18,236 --> 00:27:20,338
So Sapporo is pretty great right now.

709
00:27:20,405 --> 00:27:23,141
As an appetizer,
I'll serve chilled noodles

710
00:27:23,208 --> 00:27:26,111
you can dip in sesame sauce,
a Japanese cold <i>somen</i> dish.

711
00:27:26,177 --> 00:27:29,781
Next, you had some lamb,
so I'll grind it and make lamb <i>hambagu</i>,

712
00:27:29,848 --> 00:27:32,217
Japanese-style lamb hamburger steak.

713
00:27:32,283 --> 00:27:35,587
Lastly, soup curry is famous there.

714
00:27:35,654 --> 00:27:38,490
-Even soup curry?
-Three dishes?

715
00:27:38,556 --> 00:27:41,826
I'll use the <i>bugeo-guk</i> broth

716
00:27:41,893 --> 00:27:44,062
-you got from Mugyo-dong,
-As the base?

717
00:27:44,129 --> 00:27:46,631
…and make a rich soup curry.

718
00:27:47,732 --> 00:27:49,200
That sounds so delicious.

719
00:27:50,035 --> 00:27:52,103
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
PORK BELLY, SHRIMP, MANILA CLAM

720
00:27:52,170 --> 00:27:54,205
CILANTRO, AVOCADO, TORTILLA,
WHOLE TOMATO, CABBAGE, BAY LEAF, AND MORE

721
00:27:54,272 --> 00:27:57,709
Chef Jong-won, what will you make
to fight against him?

722
00:27:57,776 --> 00:28:00,178
As I listened to Dae-myung talk,

723
00:28:00,245 --> 00:28:02,414
he seemed to love Mexican food.

724
00:28:02,480 --> 00:28:04,949
In Mexico, there's an area
called Baja California,

725
00:28:05,016 --> 00:28:09,821
which is a long peninsula
in the northwestern part.

726
00:28:09,888 --> 00:28:12,691
There is lots of great seafood there,
so I want you to eat this

727
00:28:12,757 --> 00:28:15,026
and visit the country yourself.

728
00:28:15,093 --> 00:28:16,361
That's why my dish is called

729
00:28:16,428 --> 00:28:18,129
You Go to Mexico.

730
00:28:18,196 --> 00:28:20,498
-You Go to Mexico.
-You Go to Mexico.

731
00:28:20,565 --> 00:28:22,200
-You Go to Mexico.
-Yes.

732
00:28:22,267 --> 00:28:24,769
It's You Go to Mexico,
not, "You go to Hawaii."

733
00:28:24,836 --> 00:28:26,104
I'll make tacos

734
00:28:26,171 --> 00:28:29,474
and something called pozole,
which is Mexican soup.

735
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:32,377
I think it's similar
to <i>sundae-guk</i> in Korea.

736
00:28:32,444 --> 00:28:36,815
So I'll make that soup
with pork belly and <i>naengmyeon</i> broth.

737
00:28:36,881 --> 00:28:39,017
Also, there's a cocktail called michelada.

738
00:28:39,084 --> 00:28:41,186
It's uniquely made with beer

739
00:28:41,252 --> 00:28:43,655
-and clam juice.
-What?

740
00:28:43,722 --> 00:28:46,091
-Clam juice?
-I want you to imagine

741
00:28:46,157 --> 00:28:48,460
having tacos, pozole, and a cold cocktail

742
00:28:48,526 --> 00:28:50,361
as you sit and rest on the beach.

743
00:28:50,428 --> 00:28:52,797
I want to help you experience
that vacation spot vibe.

744
00:28:52,864 --> 00:28:54,332
-Gosh.
-Chef Jong-won.

745
00:28:54,399 --> 00:28:57,736
What's this
about you almost changing your name?

746
00:28:57,802 --> 00:29:00,505
As I mentioned, I really love tacos,

747
00:29:00,572 --> 00:29:02,507
so I considered changing my name

748
00:29:02,574 --> 00:29:05,643
-from Son Jong-won to Son Tak-ho.
-Son Tak-ho?

749
00:29:05,710 --> 00:29:07,078
-Tak-ho.
-Son Tak-ho.

750
00:29:07,145 --> 00:29:10,715
Not only is he changing his hometown,
but he's now changing his name.

751
00:29:10,782 --> 00:29:13,685
HE HAS COME A LONG WAY

752
00:29:13,752 --> 00:29:16,121
-He's no joke with his words now.
-Chef Ho-young.

753
00:29:16,187 --> 00:29:18,523
Shouldn't you think of a new name too?

754
00:29:18,590 --> 00:29:20,792
I also love

755
00:29:20,859 --> 00:29:23,394
-Sapporo very much.
-Yes?

756
00:29:23,461 --> 00:29:25,163
So how about Jung Ppo-ro?

757
00:29:25,930 --> 00:29:29,334
-Good job! You have to keep going with it.
-That's what you do.

758
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:32,237
-It's Son Tak-ho versus Jung Ppo-ro.
-Jung Ppo-ro.

759
00:29:32,303 --> 00:29:35,240
-Tak-ho versus Ppo-ro.
-Sounds great.

760
00:29:35,306 --> 00:29:39,944
Then now, let us begin
the cook-off between these two.

761
00:29:40,011 --> 00:29:41,713
<i>"Ogenki desu ka?"</i>

762
00:29:41,780 --> 00:29:46,651
Here to send Dae-myung to Sapporo,
Chef Jung Ppo-ro is up.

763
00:29:46,718 --> 00:29:48,586
"You go to Mexico."

764
00:29:48,653 --> 00:29:51,256
Chef Son Tak-ho is also here

765
00:29:51,322 --> 00:29:54,125
to send Dae-myung to a beach in Mexico.

766
00:29:54,192 --> 00:29:56,027
Will it be Japan or Mexico?

767
00:29:56,094 --> 00:29:59,397
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

768
00:30:00,198 --> 00:30:01,699
Jung Ppo-ro and Son Tak-ho.

769
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:05,403
SON TAK-HO VS. JUNG PPO-RO

770
00:30:05,470 --> 00:30:06,805
MANILA CLAM MEAT

771
00:30:06,871 --> 00:30:08,573
He's making clam stock first.

772
00:30:08,640 --> 00:30:11,910
It makes no sense
to mix clam juice with beer.

773
00:30:11,976 --> 00:30:13,745
-Nam-no, I need your help.
-Yes, sir.

774
00:30:13,812 --> 00:30:16,848
Take this and crush these.

775
00:30:16,915 --> 00:30:18,983
His kitchen toolman is helping out.

776
00:30:19,417 --> 00:30:21,486
CHILI PEPPER, GARLIC

777
00:30:21,753 --> 00:30:22,587
CHILI PEPPER & GARLIC

778
00:30:22,654 --> 00:30:24,722
-And just crush these?
-Crush them.

779
00:30:24,956 --> 00:30:26,391
ONION

780
00:30:26,457 --> 00:30:28,726
-Nam-no does his best to help.
-These too, Nam-no.

781
00:30:29,227 --> 00:30:30,595
ONION

782
00:30:30,662 --> 00:30:31,930
I'm making guacamole,

783
00:30:31,996 --> 00:30:34,833
so he's crushing chili pepper,
garlic, onion, and cilantro.

784
00:30:34,899 --> 00:30:37,569
-Pepper and garlic. It's tedious work.
-The aroma

785
00:30:37,635 --> 00:30:40,305
is completely different
when the ingredients are mashed.

786
00:30:40,371 --> 00:30:42,974
-It changes the texture and the aroma.
-And the texture.

787
00:30:43,274 --> 00:30:44,509
HOW'S TEAM SAPPORO DOING?

788
00:30:44,576 --> 00:30:46,411
-One second.
-What do I do?

789
00:30:46,477 --> 00:30:47,545
Not yet.

790
00:30:47,612 --> 00:30:49,247
-Not yet?
-No.

791
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:51,583
1. MAKE MIXTURE FOR LAMB PATTY

792
00:30:51,649 --> 00:30:54,185
BY GRINDING LAMB, ONION,
EGG WHITE, AND OYSTER SAUCE

793
00:30:54,886 --> 00:30:56,921
-Ho-young's grinding something.
-Lamb patty.

794
00:30:56,988 --> 00:30:59,724
It's lamb, oyster sauce, and onion.

795
00:30:59,791 --> 00:31:02,527
Can you keep us updated as his toolman?

796
00:31:02,594 --> 00:31:03,528
Sure thing.

797
00:31:03,595 --> 00:31:04,929
ACCEPTS HIS FATE AS TOOLMAN

798
00:31:04,996 --> 00:31:06,965
Put this and this in here

799
00:31:07,031 --> 00:31:08,132
and blend it for me.

800
00:31:08,199 --> 00:31:10,435
-One <i>tsuyu</i>. And?
-Yes.

801
00:31:10,501 --> 00:31:12,437
One <i>tsuyu</i>, one soy milk.

802
00:31:12,503 --> 00:31:15,306
A spoonful of peanut butter.

803
00:31:15,373 --> 00:31:16,641
Put them together

804
00:31:16,708 --> 00:31:17,876
and blend.

805
00:31:18,142 --> 00:31:20,111
MAKING USE OF HIS TOOLMAN

806
00:31:20,178 --> 00:31:22,413
I think he's making the sauce.

807
00:31:23,248 --> 00:31:25,283
Look at the sidekicks.

808
00:31:25,350 --> 00:31:27,285
-The kitchen tool match.
-The toolmen.

809
00:31:27,352 --> 00:31:30,154
-He's using lamb now.
-Lamb.

810
00:31:30,221 --> 00:31:34,125
Chef Ho-young makes curry.

811
00:31:34,192 --> 00:31:38,196
-From Mugyo-dong.
-You were planning to eat that later,

812
00:31:38,263 --> 00:31:39,864
-and he's using it as broth.
-Yes.

813
00:31:39,931 --> 00:31:42,100
-I wonder what that's like.
-Did you add the curry block?

814
00:31:42,166 --> 00:31:45,169
-Yes, and a bit of garam masala.
-The curry block?

815
00:31:45,236 --> 00:31:46,838
Garam masala. Wow.

816
00:31:46,905 --> 00:31:51,042
The moment you add it,
it takes you abroad, to India.

817
00:31:51,276 --> 00:31:52,810
TAK-HO CAN ALSO FOCUS THANKS TO NAM-NO

818
00:31:53,444 --> 00:31:54,279
PORK BELLY

819
00:31:56,648 --> 00:31:58,149
CRUSHED GARLIC, TEQUILA, SALSA VERDE

820
00:31:58,216 --> 00:31:59,817
He just added rice.

821
00:31:59,884 --> 00:32:01,686
Yes, he just added rice to the pot.

822
00:32:01,753 --> 00:32:02,787
Tomato?

823
00:32:02,854 --> 00:32:04,923
-He added rice and sauce to the pot.
-Bay leaf.

824
00:32:04,989 --> 00:32:06,357
What are you making, Tak-ho?

825
00:32:06,424 --> 00:32:08,293
I'm making pozole,

826
00:32:08,359 --> 00:32:11,429
which is pretty much
Mexico's favorite soup.

827
00:32:11,496 --> 00:32:13,765
It's usually made with beans or corn,

828
00:32:13,831 --> 00:32:14,899
but I'm using instant rice.

829
00:32:14,966 --> 00:32:17,302
-Pozole.
-It's like <i>gukbap</i>, rice soup.

830
00:32:18,636 --> 00:32:20,004
Is that his <i>naengmyeon </i>broth?

831
00:32:20,071 --> 00:32:21,372
I just added that.

832
00:32:21,439 --> 00:32:22,974
-He's using <i>naengmyeon </i>broth.
-Yes.

833
00:32:23,041 --> 00:32:26,344
-Son Tak uses <i>naengmyeon </i>broth.
-"Son Tak?"

834
00:32:26,411 --> 00:32:28,413
-Ppo-ro uses…
-<i>Bugeo-guk</i> soup.

835
00:32:28,479 --> 00:32:30,682
<i>Naengmyeon</i> broth versus <i>bugeo-guk</i> soup.

836
00:32:30,748 --> 00:32:32,617
It has to be better
than the original broths.

837
00:32:33,418 --> 00:32:34,719
It smells so good.

838
00:32:34,786 --> 00:32:36,621
You have to use a mortar
to maximize the aroma.

839
00:32:37,188 --> 00:32:38,122
Should I continue?

840
00:32:38,189 --> 00:32:39,557
Add avocado to it.

841
00:32:39,857 --> 00:32:42,226
A MUST FOR MEXICAN FOOD, AVOCADO!

842
00:32:42,527 --> 00:32:43,928
MASHING AVOCADO TOO

843
00:32:44,195 --> 00:32:46,164
HE'S CHECKING THE SAUCE…

844
00:32:46,230 --> 00:32:48,166
It's too salty. More soy milk.

845
00:32:48,733 --> 00:32:49,968
-One more soy milk?
-Yes.

846
00:32:51,102 --> 00:32:52,570
BLENDING AGAIN

847
00:32:52,637 --> 00:32:53,604
Is this okay?

848
00:32:53,671 --> 00:32:55,006
One more time. Some more soy milk.

849
00:32:55,073 --> 00:32:57,141
There's no more. That was it.
I used everything.

850
00:32:57,208 --> 00:32:58,476
Then water it is.

851
00:32:58,743 --> 00:33:00,278
PUTS CHEF SOMEONE UP FOR THE JOB…

852
00:33:00,578 --> 00:33:01,879
AND GOES BACK TO COOKING

853
00:33:02,714 --> 00:33:03,548
ADDING CILANTRO!

854
00:33:03,614 --> 00:33:06,117
-Are you mixing cilantro and lamb?
-Yes, it's cilantro.

855
00:33:06,184 --> 00:33:07,752
That sounds delicious.

856
00:33:07,819 --> 00:33:09,387
There's the lamb patty.

857
00:33:09,454 --> 00:33:11,155
That looks so good.

858
00:33:11,856 --> 00:33:15,059
Chef Ho-young's <i>hambagu </i>is to die for.

859
00:33:15,126 --> 00:33:17,128
-It's guaranteed to be good.
-Yes.

860
00:33:17,195 --> 00:33:20,298
You have about nine minutes left.
Six minutes have gone by.

861
00:33:20,365 --> 00:33:21,933
1. MIX FLOUR, BEER, EGG TO MAKE BATTER

862
00:33:24,035 --> 00:33:25,336
Wait, what's that?

863
00:33:25,403 --> 00:33:26,537
<i>Ramyeon</i> soup base?

864
00:33:26,604 --> 00:33:29,540
-What? <i>Ramyeon</i> soup base?
-He's using it out in the open.

865
00:33:29,607 --> 00:33:32,010
-What am I supposed to do now?
-Everyone uses it now.

866
00:33:32,076 --> 00:33:33,644
All these chefs are doing that.

867
00:33:33,711 --> 00:33:37,582
It's not like that, actually.
Mexican food requires ancho chile pepper,

868
00:33:37,648 --> 00:33:39,350
and the closest thing to that

869
00:33:39,417 --> 00:33:41,719
was this <i>ramyeon </i>soup base, in my opinion.

870
00:33:41,786 --> 00:33:43,354
It can give that flavor.

871
00:33:43,421 --> 00:33:45,890
-I need that smoky flavor.
-That's true.

872
00:33:45,957 --> 00:33:48,059
I guess it's not a bad idea
to use it adequately.

873
00:33:48,126 --> 00:33:52,030
Chef Tak-ho is
currently deep-frying shrimp.

874
00:33:52,096 --> 00:33:53,798
Tacos are often made with fried fish,

875
00:33:54,699 --> 00:33:56,200
but I'll use shrimp today.

876
00:33:56,768 --> 00:33:58,169
Chef Jong-won, I'll add these too.

877
00:33:58,236 --> 00:33:59,103
Whoa.

878
00:33:59,170 --> 00:34:01,039
He might as well
go behind the kitchen counter.

879
00:34:01,105 --> 00:34:02,407
Did you say something, Sam?

880
00:34:02,473 --> 00:34:05,410
-That he might as well go join him.
-At this rate, right?

881
00:34:05,476 --> 00:34:07,478
Control yourself, Nam-no.

882
00:34:07,545 --> 00:34:08,913
-Sit down, Nam-no.
-Sit down.

883
00:34:08,980 --> 00:34:10,948
You are not allowed to show your bums.

884
00:34:11,015 --> 00:34:13,351
I never lifted my bottoms.

885
00:34:13,684 --> 00:34:14,986
YEAH, GOOD JOB…

886
00:34:15,053 --> 00:34:18,389
-Even the toolmen are bickering.
-I guess.

887
00:34:18,456 --> 00:34:20,825
Are you helping more?
Or am I helping more?

888
00:34:20,892 --> 00:34:22,393
Telling each other to keep it moderate.

889
00:34:22,460 --> 00:34:25,496
Jong-won's toolman
finished making guacamole.

890
00:34:25,963 --> 00:34:26,798
LEMON JUICE

891
00:34:27,065 --> 00:34:27,899
SALT

892
00:34:27,965 --> 00:34:30,034
That guacamole looks so good.

893
00:34:30,101 --> 00:34:31,269
Am I done here?

894
00:34:31,335 --> 00:34:32,770
-You are.
-Okay.

895
00:34:32,837 --> 00:34:34,172
THE TOOLMAN IS DONE, NOW WHAT?

896
00:34:34,238 --> 00:34:35,406
I'll have some soda.

897
00:34:36,174 --> 00:34:37,742
GLUG GLUG

898
00:34:38,376 --> 00:34:40,344
FROM A TOOLMAN TO A MUKBANGER

899
00:34:40,545 --> 00:34:42,113
HOW ABOUT MR. JUNG PPO-RO?

900
00:34:42,313 --> 00:34:43,815
MINI BELL PEPPERS

901
00:34:43,881 --> 00:34:46,017
AND SHRIMPS

902
00:34:46,083 --> 00:34:47,752
Take these.

903
00:34:47,819 --> 00:34:49,454
-Alternate between them.
-Okay.

904
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:50,755
Like this?

905
00:34:50,822 --> 00:34:53,491
LAUGHING AT THE OTHER TOOLMAN

906
00:34:53,558 --> 00:34:55,760
Don't you think doing this

907
00:34:55,827 --> 00:34:58,062
is the same as cooking over there?

908
00:34:58,262 --> 00:34:59,297
THAT'S WHAT WE'RE SAYING

909
00:34:59,363 --> 00:35:00,898
What are you up to, Sam?

910
00:35:00,965 --> 00:35:02,934
He told me to put these on the sticks.

911
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:04,635
-Like skewers?
-Yes.

912
00:35:05,403 --> 00:35:06,471
WHAT'S HO-YOUNG DOING NOW?

913
00:35:06,537 --> 00:35:07,672
-Noodles?
-Noodles.

914
00:35:07,738 --> 00:35:10,108
Why did he bundle them up in the center?

915
00:35:10,174 --> 00:35:13,644
If you wet the tip and hold it tightly,

916
00:35:13,711 --> 00:35:16,614
the noodles will keep their shape
as they're cooked.

917
00:35:16,681 --> 00:35:17,849
You can do that?

918
00:35:18,082 --> 00:35:18,950
AMAZED

919
00:35:19,016 --> 00:35:20,585
Look, Ho-young.

920
00:35:20,651 --> 00:35:21,819
-Is this it?
-Good.

921
00:35:22,186 --> 00:35:23,020
HANDING OVER SKEWERS

922
00:35:23,087 --> 00:35:26,858
Chef Ho-young now cooks the skewers
which his toolman

923
00:35:26,924 --> 00:35:28,793
put together with care.

924
00:35:28,860 --> 00:35:30,795
They look great.

925
00:35:31,395 --> 00:35:32,363
Am I done?

926
00:35:32,430 --> 00:35:34,198
Yes, you can relax.

927
00:35:34,265 --> 00:35:36,501
You have six minutes and ten seconds.

928
00:35:37,635 --> 00:35:39,337
He's making tacos.

929
00:35:39,804 --> 00:35:42,573
HE'S JULIENNING TORTILLAS?

930
00:35:43,107 --> 00:35:45,009
Oddly, Chef Jong-won

931
00:35:45,076 --> 00:35:47,411
just julienned the taco shells.

932
00:35:47,645 --> 00:35:48,613
PULLING OUT FRIED SHRIMPS

933
00:35:48,679 --> 00:35:50,281
-Really?
-Look at the fritters.

934
00:35:50,348 --> 00:35:51,983
They look great.

935
00:35:52,049 --> 00:35:54,619
-He cut them like that?
-I think he's deep-frying those.

936
00:35:54,685 --> 00:35:56,287
Yes, it's for the soup.

937
00:35:56,354 --> 00:35:58,022
-The garnish on top.
-Yes.

938
00:35:58,589 --> 00:36:01,025
4. MAKE SALSA WITH ONION,
CHEONGYANG PEPPER, WHOLE TOMATO, CILANTRO

939
00:36:01,092 --> 00:36:02,793
That salsa looks so good.

940
00:36:02,860 --> 00:36:04,862
Will his dishes
taste authentically Mexican?

941
00:36:04,929 --> 00:36:07,765
Tortillas burn easily,
so you'd better check.

942
00:36:07,832 --> 00:36:09,200
I'm keeping my eyes on it.

943
00:36:09,267 --> 00:36:11,736
What he's making surely looks good.

944
00:36:12,136 --> 00:36:13,371
JUNG PPO-RO STEPS UP!

945
00:36:13,938 --> 00:36:15,840
-That looks good.
-It smells great.

946
00:36:15,907 --> 00:36:19,610
Latin American style
is different from Asian style.

947
00:36:19,677 --> 00:36:21,712
This one. The soup curry.

948
00:36:21,779 --> 00:36:22,980
What does it taste like?

949
00:36:23,047 --> 00:36:24,549
It's like curry but soup.

950
00:36:24,615 --> 00:36:25,883
-Have you had it?
-Of course.

951
00:36:25,950 --> 00:36:27,351
-In Sapporo?
-Yes, two, three times.

952
00:36:27,418 --> 00:36:29,520
Then you can make a comparison.

953
00:36:29,587 --> 00:36:31,989
He has had it in Sapporo.

954
00:36:32,056 --> 00:36:34,625
-Have you been to Mexico?
-I have not.

955
00:36:34,692 --> 00:36:35,693
That means

956
00:36:35,760 --> 00:36:38,162
you wouldn't know,
no matter how the dishes turn out.

957
00:36:38,429 --> 00:36:39,864
GOOD NEWS

958
00:36:39,931 --> 00:36:41,332
-He can't confirm.
-He has the upper hand.

959
00:36:41,399 --> 00:36:43,134
It's authentic Mexican.

960
00:36:43,201 --> 00:36:45,469
-He says it's authentic Mexican.
-It's authentic.

961
