00:45:41,004 --> 00:45:43,140
AGAIN, HE'S INSANE

1205
00:45:43,207 --> 00:45:46,710
So which country will I end up going to?

1206
00:45:46,777 --> 00:45:49,246
Will it be Mexico or Japan?

1207
00:45:49,313 --> 00:45:51,648
-Next, soup curry.
-Let me try the soup curry now.

1208
00:45:51,715 --> 00:45:53,984
First, try the shrimp skewer.

1209
00:45:54,051 --> 00:45:57,454
Then have some rice with the curry
with a piece of <i>hambagu</i> on top.

1210
00:45:57,721 --> 00:45:59,523
GOSH…

1211
00:45:59,857 --> 00:46:01,425
I can already picture it in my head.

1212
00:46:03,227 --> 00:46:04,995
SHRIMP SKEWERS SOAKED IN SOUP CURRY

1213
00:46:05,062 --> 00:46:06,730
Jeez. That looks so good.

1214
00:46:06,797 --> 00:46:07,965
It's still steaming.

1215
00:46:08,031 --> 00:46:09,333
EATS THE BELL PEPPER AND SHRIMP AT ONCE

1216
00:46:10,667 --> 00:46:12,402
It must be hot.

1217
00:46:12,669 --> 00:46:13,504
SMACKING LIPS

1218
00:46:13,570 --> 00:46:14,772
And now…

1219
00:46:14,838 --> 00:46:16,173
That mix is a win.

1220
00:46:16,240 --> 00:46:18,342
You can top it with curry as you like.

1221
00:46:18,408 --> 00:46:20,878
-There's meat in the curry too.
-There is.

1222
00:46:20,944 --> 00:46:22,946
-It's lamb.
-There's lamb in there?

1223
00:46:23,013 --> 00:46:25,149
The curry tasted different.

1224
00:46:26,250 --> 00:46:27,584
I can't deal with this anymore.

1225
00:46:28,285 --> 00:46:30,154
ON TOP OF EVERYTHING

1226
00:46:31,722 --> 00:46:33,423
-He loves cilantro.
-That's what I call a trip.

1227
00:46:33,490 --> 00:46:34,658
I agree.

1228
00:46:34,725 --> 00:46:36,894
THE VISUAL MAKES EVERYONE SHUDDER

1229
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:39,563
He'll try it like that.

1230
00:46:39,630 --> 00:46:41,999
-Soup curry and…
-Like this.

1231
00:46:42,065 --> 00:46:43,600
-My mouth is watering.
-It looks good.

1232
00:46:43,667 --> 00:46:44,868
Rice and cilantro.

1233
00:46:46,570 --> 00:46:47,971
<i>How amazing!</i>

1234
00:46:48,605 --> 00:46:50,808
AS IF HE'S ACTUALLY IN JAPAN

1235
00:46:54,511 --> 00:46:56,380
<i>Master, thank you.</i>

1236
00:46:56,880 --> 00:46:57,848
APPROVED AS CURRY MASTER

1237
00:46:57,915 --> 00:46:58,749
<i>Thank you.</i>

1238
00:46:58,816 --> 00:47:01,819
-You've had soup curry in Sapporo.
-Yes.

1239
00:47:01,885 --> 00:47:03,320
Does it taste similar?

1240
00:47:03,387 --> 00:47:05,622
The flavor is much more profound.

1241
00:47:05,689 --> 00:47:07,791
The soup curry I had in Sapporo

1242
00:47:07,858 --> 00:47:09,927
was far thinner than this.

1243
00:47:09,993 --> 00:47:11,061
-Was it thin?
-It was.

1244
00:47:11,128 --> 00:47:12,362
-Yes.
-It's like watery soup.

1245
00:47:12,429 --> 00:47:15,165
It's kind of like Pyongyang <i>naengmyeon</i>,
because you're mostly unsure

1246
00:47:15,232 --> 00:47:17,034
until you're addicted by the end.

1247
00:47:17,100 --> 00:47:19,136
But this hits me directly with flavors.

1248
00:47:19,203 --> 00:47:21,438
It's like, "I am soup curry!"

1249
00:47:21,505 --> 00:47:23,574
BOOMBALAMB HAS A STRONG PRESENCE

1250
00:47:23,640 --> 00:47:24,675
<i>Oishiteru.</i>

1251
00:47:24,741 --> 00:47:25,809
<i>Oishiteru?</i>

1252
00:47:25,876 --> 00:47:27,711
Isn't it <i>aishiteru</i>? Meaning "I love you?"

1253
00:47:27,778 --> 00:47:30,948
-It's lovely and delicious.
-<i>Oishii</i> plus <i>aishiteru. Oishiteru</i>.

1254
00:47:31,014 --> 00:47:33,050
<i>Become delicious!</i>

1255
00:47:34,484 --> 00:47:36,854
This is so delicious.

1256
00:47:36,920 --> 00:47:40,257
-I'm about to tear up.
-He's about to cry.

1257
00:47:40,324 --> 00:47:42,192
Tears are gushing!

1258
00:47:42,259 --> 00:47:44,094
Gushing!

1259
00:47:45,062 --> 00:47:48,732
-Why don't you go to Mexico now?
-Shall we go to Mexico?

1260
00:47:48,799 --> 00:47:53,437
This time, please try
Chef Son Tak-ho's You Go to Mexico.

1261
00:47:53,770 --> 00:47:56,273
AUTHENTIC TACOS ON THE BEACH IN MEXICO!

1262
00:47:57,541 --> 00:48:00,310
MEXICAN SOUP, MEXICAN COCKTAIL,
YOGURT SOUR CREAM

1263
00:48:01,245 --> 00:48:03,780
CHEF SON TAK-HO'S YOU GO TO MEXICO

1264
00:48:04,414 --> 00:48:07,451
-Thank you. I'll have a bite.
-He's trying the taco.

1265
00:48:08,285 --> 00:48:10,921
-Dip it in the sauce.
-In the sauce.

1266
00:48:11,922 --> 00:48:13,690
<i>One, two, three, four.</i>

1267
00:48:15,025 --> 00:48:17,561
You're now in Mexico.

1268
00:48:17,628 --> 00:48:19,029
USING EVERY SPANISH WORD HE KNOWS

1269
00:48:19,096 --> 00:48:20,163
Rodriguez!

1270
00:48:21,265 --> 00:48:22,699
-Rodriguez!
-Rodriguez!

1271
00:48:23,333 --> 00:48:25,535
DIPS IT IN THE SOUR CREAM
AND TAKES A BIG BITE

1272
00:48:26,803 --> 00:48:27,804
He's digging in.

1273
00:48:27,871 --> 00:48:30,040
ENJOYING THE TACO FLAVOR
FILLING UP HIS MOUTH

1274
00:48:30,107 --> 00:48:31,408
Oh, gosh.

1275
00:48:31,475 --> 00:48:33,911
-Is it good?
-<i>Thank you so much.</i>

1276
00:48:34,945 --> 00:48:37,514
<i>You're welcome.</i>

1277
00:48:38,649 --> 00:48:40,083
"Thank you so much."

1278
00:48:40,150 --> 00:48:41,418
THANK YOU FOR TRANSLATING

1279
00:48:41,485 --> 00:48:43,387
I got hooked on tacos

1280
00:48:43,453 --> 00:48:47,257
and did a tour around Seoul
to visit every famous taco joint.

1281
00:48:47,324 --> 00:48:50,460
There's a place in Sangsu
called G restaurant,

1282
00:48:50,527 --> 00:48:53,030
which is my favorite.

1283
00:48:53,096 --> 00:48:56,266
I've heard that their food is
similar to what they have in Mexico.

1284
00:48:56,333 --> 00:48:57,601
It's more delicious than that.

1285
00:48:59,536 --> 00:49:02,339
-José's recipe worked.
-That's José.

1286
00:49:02,406 --> 00:49:04,708
The sour flavor of this sauce

1287
00:49:04,775 --> 00:49:07,144
balances out the fried shrimp

1288
00:49:07,210 --> 00:49:10,380
and creates a perfect harmony.

1289
00:49:10,447 --> 00:49:12,616
Gosh, it's so good.
What should I eat next?

1290
00:49:12,683 --> 00:49:16,019
-Try a sip of michelada.
-The cocktail.

1291
00:49:16,086 --> 00:49:17,854
A cocktail made with clam juice.

1292
00:49:17,921 --> 00:49:22,326
That's a great idea. Glasses usually
get rimmed with salt for tequila, right?

1293
00:49:22,392 --> 00:49:24,261
-<i>Ramyeon </i>soup base would be good too.
-The soup base.

1294
00:49:24,328 --> 00:49:25,729
This might be even better.

1295
00:49:25,796 --> 00:49:27,798
Will it go well with the cocktail?

1296
00:49:30,167 --> 00:49:32,035
GULPING DOWN

1297
00:49:32,102 --> 00:49:33,704
A cocktail and <i>ramyeon </i>soup base.

1298
00:49:34,204 --> 00:49:35,439
He used clam broth, right?

1299
00:49:35,505 --> 00:49:37,040
-And beer.
-Yes, and beer.

1300
00:49:37,107 --> 00:49:38,175
Do you like it?

1301
00:49:38,241 --> 00:49:39,443
That's it.

1302
00:49:39,509 --> 00:49:40,677
What is it?

1303
00:49:40,744 --> 00:49:42,079
It's made with tomato,

1304
00:49:42,145 --> 00:49:45,983
but it's very sour,
about twice as much as ketchup.

1305
00:49:46,583 --> 00:49:51,288
And when the umami from
the <i>ramyeon </i>soup base meets the drink,

1306
00:49:51,355 --> 00:49:54,358
-it becomes the flavor of the sun.
-Flavor of the sun.

1307
00:49:54,424 --> 00:49:57,494
-He's talking about Sun Chips!
-Does it feel like…

1308
00:49:57,561 --> 00:50:00,063
-…you're tanning on the beach?
-Sun Chips?

1309
00:50:00,130 --> 00:50:02,733
There's a salsa party happening
right in front of my eyes.

1310
00:50:02,799 --> 00:50:06,436
A salsa party! It's a salsa party!

1311
00:50:06,837 --> 00:50:08,138
OH, MY…

1312
00:50:08,205 --> 00:50:09,673
-Gonzales!
-Yes!

1313
00:50:09,740 --> 00:50:11,908
<i>One, two, three, four!</i>

1314
00:50:12,909 --> 00:50:15,178
THE MEXICO VIBES ARE OFF THE WALL

1315
00:50:15,245 --> 00:50:16,580
Did you drink during the break?

1316
00:50:16,646 --> 00:50:17,914
Whatever! Just keep going!

1317
00:50:18,415 --> 00:50:19,816
GONZALES LOOKS VERY EXCITED

1318
00:50:19,883 --> 00:50:21,952
You're giving great vibes.

1319
00:50:22,019 --> 00:50:23,320
Great Mexican vibes.

1320
00:50:23,587 --> 00:50:27,090
WE'RE IN MEXICO RIGHT NOW

1321
00:50:27,157 --> 00:50:28,425
Oh, man.

1322
00:50:28,492 --> 00:50:30,927
-Is this next?
-Yes, but wait.

1323
00:50:30,994 --> 00:50:32,696
I wanted to let you enjoy it chilled,

1324
00:50:32,763 --> 00:50:35,966
so I got the tequila you love
from your freezer.

1325
00:50:36,033 --> 00:50:38,502
Let me serve it in the smaller glass

1326
00:50:38,568 --> 00:50:41,605
-with a hint of Korea.
-He had served another cup.

1327
00:50:41,671 --> 00:50:43,640
Tequila is gushing out!

1328
00:50:45,442 --> 00:50:47,310
POONG HAS COME UNDONE

1329
00:50:47,377 --> 00:50:50,380
-He's totally insane today.
-He's out of his mind.

1330
00:50:50,447 --> 00:50:53,283
Tequila gushes out.

1331
00:50:53,350 --> 00:50:55,085
I can't help but grab my passport.

1332
00:50:56,653 --> 00:50:58,488
He was on his way to Sapporo.

1333
00:50:58,555 --> 00:50:59,756
-Gosh.
-Oh, no.

1334
00:50:59,823 --> 00:51:03,827
Have a bite of the Mexican <i>gukbap</i>, pozole,

1335
00:51:03,894 --> 00:51:06,430
and then take the tequila shot,
as if it's soju.

1336
00:51:06,496 --> 00:51:08,632
Mexico's household soup, pozole.

1337
00:51:12,469 --> 00:51:14,438
CLOSES HIS EYES AND SAVORS THE FLAVOR

1338
00:51:14,504 --> 00:51:16,373
-Looks good.
-What is it like?

1339
00:51:16,440 --> 00:51:17,574
He used rice to make it.

1340
00:51:18,542 --> 00:51:20,610
He already felt a hint of Mexico earlier.

1341
00:51:20,677 --> 00:51:22,979
The tequila!

1342
00:51:24,281 --> 00:51:25,816
EXHILARATING

1343
00:51:26,483 --> 00:51:29,519
The umami from the soup hides in my mouth,

1344
00:51:29,586 --> 00:51:31,888
and the moment tequila comes in,

1345
00:51:31,955 --> 00:51:34,224
there's a salsa party in my mouth.

1346
00:51:34,291 --> 00:51:35,792
A salsa party.

1347
00:51:35,859 --> 00:51:39,696
-A salsa party?
-There's a salsa party in his mouth.

1348
00:51:39,763 --> 00:51:41,231
It's in my mouth.

1349
00:51:41,298 --> 00:51:42,332
FEELING THE VIBES AGAIN

1350
00:51:42,399 --> 00:51:43,767
It's in his mouth.

1351
00:51:43,834 --> 00:51:45,068
<i>Amigo!</i>

1352
00:51:45,335 --> 00:51:47,037
HAPPINESS INDEX, 100

1353
00:51:47,104 --> 00:51:49,306
I'm already so happy right now,

1354
00:51:49,372 --> 00:51:52,642
-so is there a need to go to Mexico?
-Right?

1355
00:51:52,709 --> 00:51:54,778
-You can eat this right here.
-It's Mexico right here.

1356
00:51:54,845 --> 00:51:57,581
Call Poong
whenever you want to go to Mexico.

1357
00:51:58,415 --> 00:52:01,718
-I will do that.
-Chef Tak-ho's tacos can do that to you.

1358
00:52:01,952 --> 00:52:04,421
…CHUCKLE

1359
00:52:04,488 --> 00:52:05,722
Why are you smiling that big?

1360
00:52:05,789 --> 00:52:09,226
I didn't know this show was like this.

1361
00:52:09,493 --> 00:52:10,961
THE SHOW'S FULL OF VIBES

1362
00:52:11,027 --> 00:52:12,496
Why don't we let Min have a bite?

1363
00:52:12,562 --> 00:52:14,064
Me? Yes, please.

1364
00:52:14,131 --> 00:52:17,167
-It's really good.
-It is?

1365
00:52:17,234 --> 00:52:19,870
That's Chef Ho-young's dish,

1366
00:52:19,936 --> 00:52:20,804
BOOMBALAMB.

1367
00:52:20,871 --> 00:52:24,808
The noodles look so pretty,
kept so neat even after cooking.

1368
00:52:24,875 --> 00:52:29,312
-He glued the tip together.
-They usually stick to one another.

1369
00:52:31,014 --> 00:52:32,249
It's so strange.

1370
00:52:32,315 --> 00:52:33,517
-Mmm.
-So…

1371
00:52:33,583 --> 00:52:36,353
Does it taste worthy
of what Dae-myung said?

1372
00:52:36,419 --> 00:52:39,456
I can't express myself
the way that he did.

1373
00:52:39,523 --> 00:52:41,258
-You can't go that far?
-It's not that.

1374
00:52:41,324 --> 00:52:42,659
I agree with his review.

1375
00:52:43,827 --> 00:52:45,996
It tastes light, yet very delicious.

1376
00:52:46,062 --> 00:52:48,431
-Try this.
-I've been wondering what it's like.

1377
00:52:49,666 --> 00:52:50,500
It's so new.

1378
00:52:50,567 --> 00:52:51,668
AMAZED

1379
00:52:51,735 --> 00:52:54,471
-It's good. It's so good.
-Is it?

1380
00:52:54,538 --> 00:52:55,839
It's good.

1381
00:52:57,340 --> 00:52:59,075
EVERYONE IS AUTOMATICALLY AMAZED

1382
00:52:59,142 --> 00:53:00,477
I made that.

1383
00:53:01,878 --> 00:53:04,548
A BIG BITE OF THE LAMB HAMBAGU

1384
00:53:05,749 --> 00:53:07,217
Mmm!

1385
00:53:07,284 --> 00:53:08,718
This is really good.

1386
00:53:09,019 --> 00:53:10,353
I'M TELLING YOU!

1387
00:53:10,420 --> 00:53:12,956
Something about that cilantro smell.
The thing you said.

1388
00:53:13,023 --> 00:53:15,292
FULLY CONVINCED

1389
00:53:16,259 --> 00:53:18,094
Wait, over there.

1390
00:53:18,161 --> 00:53:19,629
-It's not a restaurant.
-It's amazing.

1391
00:53:19,963 --> 00:53:21,898
A BUSTLING SOUP CURRY JOINT

1392
00:53:21,965 --> 00:53:23,466
What's it like, you three?

1393
00:53:23,533 --> 00:53:25,835
-Mmm.
-The soup curry.

1394
00:53:25,902 --> 00:53:27,771
It goes really well with lamb,

1395
00:53:27,837 --> 00:53:29,773
-and the thickness is perfect.
-It is.

1396
00:53:29,839 --> 00:53:31,875
It can't be too thin or thick.

1397
00:53:31,942 --> 00:53:33,944
The curry's good,
but the noodles were amazing.

1398
00:53:34,010 --> 00:53:35,512
The Sam Kim sauce is great.

1399
00:53:35,579 --> 00:53:37,681
-I made that.
-The Sam Kim sauce?

1400
00:53:37,881 --> 00:53:39,049
APPROVED

1401
00:53:39,115 --> 00:53:42,319
As we speak,
Min's taking a bite of the taco.

1402
00:53:42,385 --> 00:53:44,254
-I'll eat it too.
-It fills his mouth.

1403
00:53:44,621 --> 00:53:46,089
WHOA

1404
00:53:46,156 --> 00:53:47,524
This has

1405
00:53:47,591 --> 00:53:49,292
a richer flavor of aroma,

1406
00:53:49,359 --> 00:53:52,862
and it hits me much harder.

1407
00:53:53,263 --> 00:53:55,699
FULL OF MEXICAN FLAVORS AND AROMAS

1408
00:53:55,765 --> 00:53:58,034
I haven't had many tacos.

1409
00:53:58,101 --> 00:54:00,804
So this is what it's like.
It's really good.

1410
00:54:00,870 --> 00:54:02,005
ADDICTED TO AUTHENTIC TACOS

1411
00:54:02,339 --> 00:54:04,074
TAKING A BIG BITE

1412
00:54:04,808 --> 00:54:06,209
Hmm?

1413
00:54:06,276 --> 00:54:07,911
Is it to die for?

1414
00:54:07,978 --> 00:54:09,045
This is good too.

1415
00:54:09,546 --> 00:54:11,748
MOUTHS FULL WITH TACOS

1416
00:54:11,815 --> 00:54:12,782
Mmm!

1417
00:54:13,483 --> 00:54:14,884
It's awfully delicious.

1418
00:54:14,951 --> 00:54:16,052
My appetite is on.

1419
00:54:16,119 --> 00:54:17,520
How will you pick, Dae-myung?

1420
00:54:17,587 --> 00:54:19,422
-What do I do?
-Oh…

1421
00:54:19,489 --> 00:54:21,992
It's now time to make a choice.

1422
00:54:22,058 --> 00:54:24,628
Choose one to go to Mexico with Son Tak-ho

1423
00:54:24,694 --> 00:54:27,297
and two to go to Japan with Jung Ppo-ro.

1424
00:54:27,364 --> 00:54:28,765
Make your decision

1425
00:54:28,832 --> 00:54:30,333
and press the button!

1426
00:54:30,400 --> 00:54:31,668
I won't be able to choose.

1427
00:54:33,403 --> 00:54:35,272
IT'S NOT AN EASY DECISION…

1428
00:54:35,605 --> 00:54:37,107
AFTER MUCH THOUGHT

1429
00:54:37,173 --> 00:54:38,975
-Okay. I did.
-Did you press one?

1430
00:54:39,042 --> 00:54:40,543
All right.

1431
00:54:40,610 --> 00:54:43,413
Which chef did Dae-myung choose?

1432
00:54:43,480 --> 00:54:45,115
Let's check out the result!

1433
00:54:45,181 --> 00:54:46,416
RESULT ANNOUNCEMENT

1434
00:54:46,483 --> 00:54:49,519
His drama wrapped,
and he wants to go on a trip.

1435
00:54:49,586 --> 00:54:51,221
Will he go to Mexico,

1436
00:54:51,288 --> 00:54:52,989
or will it be Japan?

1437
00:54:53,056 --> 00:54:54,557
Who did Dae-myung choose?

1438
00:54:54,624 --> 00:54:55,659
Which country is it?

1439
00:54:56,226 --> 00:54:58,528
Is it Japan or Mexico?

1440
00:54:58,595 --> 00:55:00,297
Jung Ppo-ro or Son Tak-ho?

1441
00:55:00,363 --> 00:55:01,498
Dae-myung's choice!

1442
00:55:02,932 --> 00:55:04,401
JUNG HO-YOUNG WINS

1443
00:55:04,467 --> 00:55:05,435
He's off to Japan!

1444
00:55:05,502 --> 00:55:07,971
CHEF JUNG PPO-RO'S BOOMBALAMB WINS

1445
00:55:08,038 --> 00:55:11,041
-Sapporo!
-To Sapporo, Jung Ppo-ro!

1446
00:55:11,107 --> 00:55:12,475
THE SAPPORO DISH CAPTURED HIM

1447
00:55:12,542 --> 00:55:15,211
-This is way too good. It's insane.
-Jung Ppo-ro wins.

1448
00:55:15,278 --> 00:55:18,581
I often go to Sapporo in winter
to see the snow.

1449
00:55:18,648 --> 00:55:19,716
-Right.
-But this dish

1450
00:55:19,783 --> 00:55:23,586
made me curious about Sapporo in summer,
something I have never seen before.

1451
00:55:25,188 --> 00:55:26,122
I see.

1452
00:55:26,189 --> 00:55:28,525
-And it's summer now.
-Agreed.

1453
00:55:28,591 --> 00:55:29,893
-Agreed.
-First off,

1454
00:55:29,959 --> 00:55:32,662
thank you so much for choosing me.

1455
00:55:32,729 --> 00:55:35,165
Sam helped me out big time too.

1456
00:55:35,398 --> 00:55:36,466
PLEASED

1457
00:55:36,533 --> 00:55:38,835
You were urging him to mention you.

1458
00:55:38,902 --> 00:55:41,438
-Shouldn't Sam get the star?
-Right?

1459
00:55:41,504 --> 00:55:42,872
I'll take it in his place.

1460
00:55:42,939 --> 00:55:45,108
He just stood up.

1461
00:55:45,175 --> 00:55:46,376
THE VERSATILE KITCHEN TOOLMAN

1462
00:55:46,443 --> 00:55:48,445
This means

1463
00:55:48,511 --> 00:55:51,448
Chef Son Tak-ho is stuck with nine stars.

1464
00:55:51,514 --> 00:55:53,817
He's been jinxed.

1465
00:55:53,883 --> 00:55:55,952
You must feel rather uneasy.

1466
00:55:56,019 --> 00:55:58,221
Here's to my friends
who are watching this in Mexico.

1467
00:55:58,288 --> 00:56:00,457
Sanchez, José,

1468
00:56:00,523 --> 00:56:02,892
-and Rodriguez.
-Didn't he imagine them?

1469
00:56:02,959 --> 00:56:04,094
I'm sorry, guys.

1470
00:56:06,062 --> 00:56:10,166
Please put a star on Chef Jung Ho-young!

1471
00:56:10,233 --> 00:56:13,803
Ho-young and Sung-jun are now in a tie!

1472
00:56:13,870 --> 00:56:17,340
This is a very significant star.

1473
00:56:17,640 --> 00:56:19,309
DAE-MYUNG ARRIVES AT SAPPORO!

1474
00:56:19,376 --> 00:56:21,311
He has arrived in Japan.

1475
00:56:22,479 --> 00:56:26,683
<i>This is the second cook-off</i>
<i>with ingredients from Dae-myung's fridge.</i>

1476
00:56:26,750 --> 00:56:30,787
Chef Choi Hyun-suk, a 55-year-old grandpa
with many stories to tell,

1477
00:56:31,521 --> 00:56:33,656
versus Chef Sam Kim,

1478
00:56:33,723 --> 00:56:35,825
who's only talkative in the green room.

1479
00:56:37,293 --> 00:56:39,562
-He's our host.
-The Yu Jae-seok of the green room.

1480
00:56:39,629 --> 00:56:40,764
He's good.

1481
00:56:40,830 --> 00:56:43,299
-This is a classic rival match.
-A classic rival match.

1482
00:56:43,366 --> 00:56:45,468
Unfitting of that grand title,

1483
00:56:45,535 --> 00:56:48,405
one of the two chefs
has had an overwhelming win.

1484
00:56:48,638 --> 00:56:50,473
WHO IS IT?

1485
00:56:50,540 --> 00:56:53,309
Chef Hyun-suk has lost

1486
00:56:53,376 --> 00:56:56,045
four rounds in a row against Chef Sam.

1487
00:56:56,346 --> 00:56:58,448
EVEN SURPRISED HIMSELF

1488
00:56:58,515 --> 00:57:01,818
-Is that why he has a lot pent up inside?
-He does?

1489
00:57:01,885 --> 00:57:03,787
-He said this in the green room.
-Ah-ha.

1490
00:57:03,853 --> 00:57:05,422
I'm sure that's the case.

1491
00:57:05,488 --> 00:57:06,856
CAN HAPPEN AFTER 4 LOSSES

1492
00:57:06,923 --> 00:57:09,893
"It's about time
people stopped calling Sam a saint."

1493
00:57:11,327 --> 00:57:12,896
"He has been corrupted."

1494
00:57:14,564 --> 00:57:16,966
-What do you mean?
-That's strange.

1495
00:57:17,033 --> 00:57:19,035
During our first season,

1496
00:57:19,102 --> 00:57:21,304
he always cooked with his philosophy,
no matter what.

1497
00:57:21,371 --> 00:57:22,305
He did.

1498
00:57:22,372 --> 00:57:25,175
-Naturalism. Healthy food.
-Yes, right. Naturalism.

1499
00:57:25,241 --> 00:57:26,476
He never compromised.

1500
00:57:26,543 --> 00:57:27,510
But that man

1501
00:57:27,577 --> 00:57:29,012
has changed since this year.

1502
00:57:29,078 --> 00:57:30,380
He's now consumed by stars.

1503
00:57:31,815 --> 00:57:34,184
He doesn't care
about the ways, means, or ingredients.

1504
00:57:34,250 --> 00:57:36,019
He throws everything in.

1505
00:57:37,153 --> 00:57:39,289
So he's now nicknamed SO Chef.

1506
00:57:39,355 --> 00:57:40,990
CHEF JONG-WON?

1507
00:57:41,057 --> 00:57:42,459
-SO Chef?
-SO Chef?

1508
00:57:42,525 --> 00:57:43,693
SO Chef?

1509
00:57:43,760 --> 00:57:46,629
This was the first time
Chef Jong-won butt into our banter.

1510
00:57:46,696 --> 00:57:49,399
-So you could do that?
-He had a lot bottled up too.

1511
00:57:49,466 --> 00:57:52,469
-He had only watched us until now.
-Right.

1512
00:57:52,535 --> 00:57:54,137
I chimed in, thinking I was funny.

1513
00:57:55,271 --> 00:57:57,273
-But it wasn't that good. Sorry.
-Nice try.

1514
00:57:57,340 --> 00:57:58,942
SO Chef wasn't so bad.

1515
00:57:59,008 --> 00:58:00,643
-Star-Obsessed Chef.
-Star-Obsessed Chef.

1516
00:58:00,710 --> 00:58:02,879
-Good try.
-SO Chef.

1517
00:58:02,946 --> 00:58:05,381
Chef Sam, there are these allegations,

1518
00:58:05,448 --> 00:58:07,650
so we'd like to hear
your official stand on this.

1519
00:58:07,717 --> 00:58:10,487
I don't know.
I wouldn't say I'm consumed by stars.

1520
00:58:10,553 --> 00:58:12,789
As the kids say, I'm following the trend.

1521
00:58:13,089 --> 00:58:14,424
NOW CORRUPT AND ELOQUENT

1522
00:58:14,491 --> 00:58:15,992
He's better with words too.

1523
00:58:16,059 --> 00:58:17,727
Just now, Chef Jong-won chiming in

1524
00:58:17,794 --> 00:58:19,395
and joining the banter

1525
00:58:19,462 --> 00:58:20,930
was so funny.

1526
00:58:20,997 --> 00:58:23,633
I feel like he has moved over…

1527
00:58:23,700 --> 00:58:26,469
…a little closer to our world.
I feel like we're closer.

1528
00:58:26,536 --> 00:58:29,172
Yes, we all grow closer over banter.

1529
00:58:29,239 --> 00:58:33,209
-So Jong-won is changing, not you?
-You're good.

1530
00:58:33,276 --> 00:58:35,111
He has changed so much.

1531
00:58:35,178 --> 00:58:36,312
You didn't expect this, right?

1532
00:58:36,379 --> 00:58:38,948
-It's very entertaining.
-The chefs jabbing, I mean.

1533
00:58:39,015 --> 00:58:40,517
THE ENTERTAINING WORLD OF BANTER

1534
00:58:40,583 --> 00:58:41,885
For your cook-off,

1535
00:58:41,951 --> 00:58:44,254
you're to make something
with a backstory of your own.

1536
00:58:45,021 --> 00:58:47,056
So you have to weave
your story into the dish,

1537
00:58:47,123 --> 00:58:49,526
and I wonder what they have in store.

1538
00:58:50,360 --> 00:58:52,395
CHEF HYUN-SUK'S INGREDIENTS:
CAPELLINI, BEEF TENDERLOIN, SHRIMP

1539
00:58:52,462 --> 00:58:54,531
WEBFOOT OCTOPUS, BASIL, PARSLEY,
OLIVE, AVOCADO, APPLE, AND MORE

1540
00:58:54,597 --> 00:58:57,500
You're up first, Chef Hyun-suk.
What are you making?

1541
00:58:58,067 --> 00:58:59,536
The dish I'll make is

1542
00:59:00,103 --> 00:59:02,038
Annals of the Choi Hyun-suk Dynasty.

1543
00:59:02,105 --> 00:59:03,873
-Annals of the Choi Hyun-suk Dynasty?
-Annals of the Choi Hyun-suk Dynasty?

1544
00:59:03,940 --> 00:59:06,576
-Not the Annals of the Joseon Dynasty.
-Not Joseon.

1545
00:59:06,643 --> 00:59:09,212
My philosophy in culinary arts

1546
00:59:09,279 --> 00:59:11,781
has formed about 21 years ago.

1547
00:59:11,848 --> 00:59:13,917
I studied under my teacher for a decade,

1548
00:59:13,983 --> 00:59:16,252
then finally opened my own place
and became the head chef.

1549
00:59:16,319 --> 00:59:18,154
And this review changed my entire career.

1550
00:59:18,221 --> 00:59:20,757
It said my food was delicious,
but most of it overlapped

1551
00:59:20,823 --> 00:59:22,859
with my previous restaurant, La Cucina.

1552
00:59:22,926 --> 00:59:24,694
So I told myself I'd make something

1553
00:59:24,761 --> 00:59:26,529
that nobody else would,

1554
00:59:26,596 --> 00:59:29,666
-and I came up with cold capellini.
-Cold capellini.

1555
00:59:29,732 --> 00:59:31,534
-His signature dish.
-Because 21 years ago,

1556
00:59:31,601 --> 00:59:35,738
no one really ate pasta cold.

1557
00:59:35,805 --> 00:59:37,540
Back then, I was nicknamed the Crazy Chef.

1558
00:59:37,607 --> 00:59:39,175
-Yes.
-And since that time,

1559
00:59:39,242 --> 00:59:41,978
I've changed my menu monthly
and never stopped inventing new dishes,

1560
00:59:42,045 --> 00:59:45,348
which developed me
into the creative chef you're seeing now

1561
00:59:45,415 --> 00:59:46,950
Today, I'd like to present

1562
00:59:47,016 --> 00:59:50,153
the chronological changes
in my history in a single dish.

1563
00:59:50,520 --> 00:59:51,487
DEEPLY MOVED♥

1564
00:59:51,554 --> 00:59:55,258
Then can we say cold capellini

1565
00:59:55,325 --> 00:59:57,093
was the first dish you created?

1566
00:59:57,160 --> 01:00:00,530
Yes, the first creative dish I made
was cold pasta.

1567
01:00:01,798 --> 01:00:05,068
There's a picture he sent us.

1568
01:00:05,134 --> 01:00:08,404
It'll help you stay immersed in his story.

1569
01:00:08,471 --> 01:00:09,973
These are his old pictures.

1570
01:00:10,807 --> 01:00:12,008
Wait a second.

1571
01:00:12,375 --> 01:00:14,077
THESE AREN'T PHOTOS OF COLD CAPELLINI

1572
01:00:14,143 --> 01:00:15,845
-No pictures of his pasta?
-That's not the dish.

1573
01:00:15,912 --> 01:00:17,880
EXCUSE ME, CHEF HYUN-SUK

1574
01:00:17,947 --> 01:00:21,784
You're supposed to show us
photos related to your restaurant.

1575
01:00:21,851 --> 01:00:23,720
-Show us the pasta.
-He's trying to show us

1576
01:00:23,786 --> 01:00:25,989
that he used to be thin like capellini.

1577
01:00:26,055 --> 01:00:28,124
He's more like penne now.

1578
01:00:29,759 --> 01:00:31,294
I'll send the pictures of the pasta

1579
01:00:31,361 --> 01:00:36,599
and let the viewers see it
by the time this episode is on air.

1580
01:00:36,666 --> 01:00:38,034
That was so random.

1581
01:00:38,101 --> 01:00:41,904
<i>I think he showed that to tell us,</i>
<i>"Stop calling me grandpa."</i>

1582
01:00:42,739 --> 01:00:44,774
CHEF SAM'S INGREDIENTS:
KIMCHI, TORTILLA, BEEF SIRLOIN

1583
01:00:44,841 --> 01:00:46,909
BEEF TENDERLOIN, BRISKET,
CHERRY TOMATO, CILANTRO, AND MORE

1584
01:00:47,477 --> 01:00:49,078
The name of my dish is

1585
01:00:49,145 --> 01:00:52,115
-Tails of Mexican Tales.
-Tails of Mexican Tales.

1586
01:00:52,181 --> 01:00:53,616
-Like the show <i>Tails of Tales</i>?
-Yes.

1587
01:00:53,683 --> 01:00:56,519
Because my tale
has many tails to go off on.

1588
01:00:56,586 --> 01:00:57,587
It's a pretty long story.

1589
01:00:57,654 --> 01:00:59,288
Tails of Mexican Tales?

1590
01:00:59,355 --> 01:01:01,290
-It's a story of my friend.
-Your friend?

1591
01:01:01,357 --> 01:01:04,093
His name is Charlo Martinez,

1592
01:01:04,160 --> 01:01:06,663
and we became friends
while working together

1593
01:01:06,729 --> 01:01:08,865
when I first started my culinary career.

1594
01:01:08,931 --> 01:01:12,001
He always made me burritos.

1595
01:01:12,068 --> 01:01:14,170
-Burritos.
-Burritos.

1596
01:01:14,237 --> 01:01:17,073
And when I made something,
I'd make kimchi fried rice.

1597
01:01:17,140 --> 01:01:20,376
He really loved
eating that with roasted seaweed.

1598
01:01:20,443 --> 01:01:21,444
That's a good mix.

1599
01:01:21,511 --> 01:01:24,113
Actually, it's a pretty long story.

1600
01:01:24,180 --> 01:01:25,415
Sam!

1601
01:01:25,481 --> 01:01:27,950
I'm sorry, but if it's a long story,
may I sit down?

1602
01:01:29,619 --> 01:01:31,454
-Sit down.
-His blood sugar is dropping.

1603
01:01:31,521 --> 01:01:34,090
-But I listened to his story!
-You're so selfish.

1604
01:01:34,157 --> 01:01:35,391
He's serious. It's so sad.

1605
01:01:35,725 --> 01:01:37,360
WE KNOW YOU'RE STILL GOING STRONG

1606
01:01:37,427 --> 01:01:39,896
-Your Mexican friend.
-Oh, yes.

1607
01:01:39,962 --> 01:01:43,533
We became very close,
and he also began to cook seriously.

1608
01:01:43,599 --> 01:01:44,934
After some time, we parted ways.

1609
01:01:45,001 --> 01:01:48,237
Fifteen years after that,
when I went back to America and met him,

1610
01:01:48,304 --> 01:01:51,307
he gave me a picture
the two of us took together.

1611
01:01:51,374 --> 01:01:52,575
I miss him a lot,

1612
01:01:52,642 --> 01:01:54,310
so I'm going to make

1613
01:01:54,377 --> 01:01:57,113
a burrito with kimchi fried rice filling.

1614
01:01:57,180 --> 01:01:58,381
-Huh?
-Kimchi burrito.

1615
01:01:58,448 --> 01:01:59,816
-Kimchi burrito.
-It sounds like

1616
01:01:59,882 --> 01:02:03,720
you suddenly came up
with Tails of Mexican Tales

1617
01:02:03,786 --> 01:02:07,223
after learning that
Dae-myung is into Mexican food.

1618
01:02:07,290 --> 01:02:08,825
You are consumed by stars.

1619
01:02:08,891 --> 01:02:10,326
Not at all.

1620
01:02:10,393 --> 01:02:12,428
I even have our picture
from when we worked together.

1621
01:02:12,495 --> 01:02:15,064
-If he has photo proof, it must be true.
-There he is.

1622
01:02:15,131 --> 01:02:16,099
A PHOTO FROM CHARLO

1623
01:02:16,165 --> 01:02:17,834
Really? Back then?

1624
01:02:17,900 --> 01:02:19,302
You had short hair?

1625
01:02:19,368 --> 01:02:21,838
-I shaved it off when I became a cook.
-I see.

1626
01:02:21,904 --> 01:02:24,774
The photo on the left
is during the 2002 World Cup season,

1627
01:02:24,841 --> 01:02:27,643
when he cheered for Korea with me

1628
01:02:27,710 --> 01:02:29,746
on the day of Jung-hwan's historic goal.

1629
01:02:29,812 --> 01:02:31,881
Sorry, but doesn't that mean
I'm why he became a chef?

1630
01:02:31,948 --> 01:02:33,282
-No.
-You're right.

1631
01:02:33,349 --> 01:02:36,385
-That's true.
-He must've taken interest in Korea.

1632
01:02:36,452 --> 01:02:38,688
You'll see the Red Devils T-shirt there.

1633
01:02:38,755 --> 01:02:40,556
-The Red Devils shirt.
-The Red Devils.

1634
01:02:40,623 --> 01:02:42,391
-That's a must.
-A must.

1635
01:02:42,458 --> 01:02:44,694
And that's the photo from a decade after.

1636
01:02:44,761 --> 01:02:47,296
-Yes, in 2024.
-Only 2 years ago.

1637
01:02:47,363 --> 01:02:50,066
-Wait, are you wearing a microphone?
-What?

1638
01:02:50,133 --> 01:02:51,734
-Were you filming? We got you.
-Yes.

1639
01:02:51,801 --> 01:02:55,371
-It was for a documentary.
-Oh, back then.

1640
01:02:55,438 --> 01:02:57,640
I think I saw him as an actor
on a reenactment show.

1641
01:02:57,707 --> 01:02:59,575
You're mistaken.

1642
01:02:59,642 --> 01:03:03,012
Well, there are a few parts
we're still suspicious of.

1643
01:03:03,679 --> 01:03:06,682
-It's odd that you're making Mexican food.
-Right?

1644
01:03:06,749 --> 01:03:10,119
That's why I want to use
my kimchi fried rice as the filling

1645
01:03:10,186 --> 01:03:12,989
and roll up a burrito
just like Charlo used to do for me.

1646
01:03:13,055 --> 01:03:14,223
A mix of two memories.

1647
01:03:14,557 --> 01:03:15,725
MEMORIES WITH HIS MEXICAN FRIEND

1648
01:03:15,792 --> 01:03:17,493
He'll think of Martinez as he cooks.

1649
01:03:17,560 --> 01:03:18,594
And now,

1650
01:03:18,661 --> 01:03:22,565
let us begin the next round,
Food, Vibe, Chefs.

1651
01:03:22,632 --> 01:03:24,467
Nicknamed the SO Chef,

1652
01:03:24,534 --> 01:03:27,737
the Star-Obsessed Chef, Sam Kim.

1653
01:03:27,804 --> 01:03:30,339
Versus another SO Chef,

1654
01:03:30,406 --> 01:03:35,311
-the Self-Obsessed Chef, Hyun-suk.
-Self-Obsessed?

1655
01:03:35,378 --> 01:03:39,515
Which one of the two will become
the storyteller to win Dae-myung's heart?

1656
01:03:39,582 --> 01:03:41,951
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1657
01:03:43,586 --> 01:03:45,221
A STORYTELLING ROUND!

1658
01:03:45,755 --> 01:03:47,123
SAM BEGINS BY WARMING UP INSTANT RICE

1659
01:03:47,190 --> 01:03:49,192
-Hoping to see some heartwarming dishes.
-What's he doing?

1660
01:03:49,258 --> 01:03:50,092
-Basil.
-Basil?

1661
01:03:50,159 --> 01:03:52,762
-I'm making basil pesto.
-Basil pesto.

1662
01:03:52,829 --> 01:03:54,897
But mine has a different recipe.

1663
01:03:54,964 --> 01:03:56,732
-Huh? How is it different?
-How is it different?

1664
01:03:56,799 --> 01:03:59,402
-I add parsley.
-Parsley.

1665
01:03:59,468 --> 01:04:03,172
Curly parsley adds
a nice green aroma and a darker color.

1666
01:04:03,239 --> 01:04:06,142
-So he adds parsley.
-It can be too strong, so not too much.

1667
01:04:06,209 --> 01:04:07,877
Are you making fresh pasta?

1668
01:04:07,944 --> 01:04:10,179
Capellini is a kind of thin pasta,

1669
01:04:10,246 --> 01:04:12,014
-so it gets soggy quickly.
-Right.

1670
01:04:12,081 --> 01:04:13,382
So I'm using dried noodles.

1671
01:04:13,449 --> 01:04:14,617
Hmm.

1672
01:04:15,251 --> 01:04:16,085
Okay.

1673
01:04:16,152 --> 01:04:17,386
DISAPPOINTED…

1674
01:04:18,387 --> 01:04:20,923
-Fresh pasta would've been more moving.
-Right?

1675
01:04:20,990 --> 01:04:23,226
-Even if it's thin.
-Yes.

1676
01:04:23,292 --> 01:04:24,861
-I mean, dried pasta?
-He makes linguine.

1677
01:04:24,927 --> 01:04:27,363
-I can't lie to make this more moving.
-There's a story here.

1678
01:04:27,430 --> 01:04:28,397
GUILTY AS CHARGED

1679
01:04:28,464 --> 01:04:29,966
That pasta may not have been around

1680
01:04:30,032 --> 01:04:32,568
-twenty-one years ago.
-Exactly.

1681
01:04:33,135 --> 01:04:35,004
It'd be great if someone could hold this.

1682
01:04:35,304 --> 01:04:36,539
THE TOOL GETS REPLACED

1683
01:04:36,606 --> 01:04:37,907
This is becoming a new fad.

1684
01:04:37,974 --> 01:04:39,876
A MUST-HAVE GADGET: KITCHEN TOOLMAN

1685
01:04:39,942 --> 01:04:41,244
Now, the SO Chef

1686
01:04:41,310 --> 01:04:44,380
-has to make a burrito.
-He does.

1687
01:04:44,447 --> 01:04:46,616
But first, kimchi fried rice.

1688
01:04:46,682 --> 01:04:48,150
Kimchi burrito sounds good.

1689
01:04:48,217 --> 01:04:50,820
1. STIR-FRY ONION, GREEN ONION,
BEEF SIRLOIN, AND MINCED GARLIC

1690
01:04:50,887 --> 01:04:51,854
That's beef.

1691
01:04:51,921 --> 01:04:53,356
-Is that sirloin?
-It is.

1692
01:04:53,623 --> 01:04:55,024
HYUN-SUK MAKES THE PASTA SAUCE!

1693
01:04:55,358 --> 01:04:57,159
OLIVE

1694
01:04:57,226 --> 01:04:59,428
Olives and anchovy paste.

1695
01:04:59,495 --> 01:05:02,064
-Olives, anchovy paste, and oil.
-Oil.

1696
01:05:02,365 --> 01:05:03,266
FINISHED!

1697
01:05:03,332 --> 01:05:04,901
I had those in my fridge.

1698
01:05:04,967 --> 01:05:06,502
No garlic in either of them?

1699
01:05:06,569 --> 01:05:08,070
None. My recipe is different.

1700
01:05:08,137 --> 01:05:09,872
-No garlic?
-No garlic.

1701
01:05:09,939 --> 01:05:12,975
I can mash it with my hand,

1702
01:05:13,042 --> 01:05:14,443
but the blender causes heat

1703
01:05:14,510 --> 01:05:15,745
and brings out the sourness

1704
01:05:15,811 --> 01:05:18,781
-from the olives.
-I see.

1705
01:05:18,848 --> 01:05:19,815
Also, originally…

1706
01:05:19,882 --> 01:05:21,884
I'm sorry, but do you know
that you only have 15 minutes?

1707
01:05:21,951 --> 01:05:22,919
CAN YOU TALK AND COOK?

1708
01:05:22,985 --> 01:05:24,453
Why is he out of breath?

1709
01:05:24,520 --> 01:05:27,156
-You're mostly just talking.
-Basil pesto is made with pine nuts…

1710
01:05:27,223 --> 01:05:29,492
-He must share his story.
-…but there were none.

1711
01:05:29,558 --> 01:05:33,996
Avocado also has lots of fat,
just like pine nuts, so I'll use that.

1712
01:05:34,063 --> 01:05:36,499
Both pine nuts and avocados
are rich in fat.

1713
01:05:36,565 --> 01:05:38,634
The avocado will act like pine nuts.

1714
01:05:38,701 --> 01:05:39,802
Like pine nuts.

1715
01:05:42,972 --> 01:05:46,175
So use avocado
to give it a texture like pine nuts.

1716
01:05:47,410 --> 01:05:48,244
ONION

1717
01:05:48,577 --> 01:05:49,412
GREEN ONION

1718
01:05:49,645 --> 01:05:50,513
CHEONGYANG PEPPER

1719
01:05:50,913 --> 01:05:54,550
2. MAKE SAUCE WITH ONION, GREEN ONION,
SOY SAUCE, PEPPER, RED PEPPER POWDER

1720
01:05:54,850 --> 01:05:56,052
AND ADD A KICK!

1721
01:05:57,320 --> 01:05:58,421
He's using parsley.

1722
01:05:58,487 --> 01:06:00,589
-Parsley.
-He's using parsley too.

1723
01:06:00,656 --> 01:06:01,857
Even the stems?

1724
01:06:01,924 --> 01:06:04,760
-He's using lots of parsley.
-He is.

1725
01:06:05,328 --> 01:06:06,529
OLIVE OIL

1726
01:06:06,595 --> 01:06:09,999
I had bought lots of parsley
to make lemon pasta.

1727
01:06:10,066 --> 01:06:11,767
I'm glad I did.

1728
01:06:12,134 --> 01:06:13,302
BUT…

1729
01:06:13,369 --> 01:06:15,304
Are you using both parsley and cilantro?

1730
01:06:17,073 --> 01:06:18,407
-Chef Sam!
-Yes?

1731
01:06:18,474 --> 01:06:20,609
No, I'm only using cilantro.

1732
01:06:20,676 --> 01:06:22,812
-It's not parsley.
-That's cilantro?

1733
01:06:22,878 --> 01:06:26,849
-It's cilantro?
-If we get something wrong, tell us so.

1734
01:06:26,916 --> 01:06:28,584
WE WERE GOING ON ABOUT PARSLEY…

1735
01:06:28,651 --> 01:06:30,853
I didn't know you were asking me.

1736
01:06:30,920 --> 01:06:33,389
You're not listening
to anything we're saying.

1737
01:06:33,456 --> 01:06:35,591
Don't just tell the story.
Listen a little too.

1738
01:06:35,658 --> 01:06:38,160
-The story almost got distorted.
-Exactly.

1739
01:06:38,227 --> 01:06:39,729
He's using cilantro now.

1740
01:06:39,795 --> 01:06:41,731
He made rice to make the burrito.

1741
01:06:41,797 --> 01:06:45,668
Sam, telling a story
begins by listening to others' stories.

1742
01:06:45,735 --> 01:06:46,769
TALKATIVE VERSUS QUIET

1743
01:06:46,836 --> 01:06:47,770
Sure.

1744
01:06:48,671 --> 01:06:51,774
He's cooking meat with green onion.

1745
01:06:51,841 --> 01:06:53,042
And a load of sugar!

1746
01:06:53,109 --> 01:06:55,077
-It's for the fried rice.
-That much sugar?

1747
01:06:55,144 --> 01:06:58,781
-Have you seen him use that much sugar?
-Chef Sam is using sugar?

1748
01:06:58,848 --> 01:07:00,883
I thought you were making kimchi jam!

1749
01:07:01,550 --> 01:07:03,252
-Chef Sam.
-Charlo liked it this way.

1750
01:07:03,319 --> 01:07:04,387
Okay. Sure.

1751
01:07:04,453 --> 01:07:05,688
All right.

1752
01:07:05,755 --> 01:07:08,491
-That's how Charlo liked it.
-I mean, he liked it.

1753
01:07:08,557 --> 01:07:11,027
-If he didn't, he wouldn't do that.
-You're right.

1754
01:07:11,093 --> 01:07:13,462
He's making it in a way that Charlo loved.

1755
01:07:13,696 --> 01:07:14,530
SALT

1756
01:07:14,864 --> 01:07:15,698
PEPPER

1757
01:07:16,766 --> 01:07:19,402
STIR-FRIED CILANTRO BRISKET
AS THE BURRITO FILLING

1758
01:07:21,037 --> 01:07:23,639
His basil pesto is done.

1759
01:07:23,706 --> 01:07:25,908
Is he going to mix the two?
Or use them separately?

1760
01:07:25,975 --> 01:07:28,911
-He's making two different sauces.
-They're different sauces.

1761
01:07:28,978 --> 01:07:30,346
Maybe he's making three.

1762
01:07:31,580 --> 01:07:34,083
-What's that?
-Truffle oil spray.

1763
01:07:34,150 --> 01:07:35,418
-Truffle oil.
-I see.

1764
01:07:35,484 --> 01:07:37,219
Truffle oil comes in a spray?

1765
01:07:37,286 --> 01:07:39,989
-Truffle oil and soy sauce.
-Those two go well together.

1766
01:07:40,056 --> 01:07:42,058
-They do.
-They go well very nicely.

1767
01:07:42,124 --> 01:07:44,226
And he already knew that 21 years ago?

1768
01:07:44,293 --> 01:07:45,761
He's something.

1769
01:07:45,828 --> 01:07:48,731
Wait, I think this is a recent technique.

1770
01:07:48,798 --> 01:07:50,433
-Is this your old technique?
-Yes.

1771
01:07:50,499 --> 01:07:51,400
-Yes.
-Really?

1772
01:07:51,467 --> 01:07:53,002
The technique from 21 years ago.

1773
01:07:53,469 --> 01:07:55,371
CHERRY TOMATOES

1774
01:07:55,838 --> 01:07:59,508
4. MIX CHERRY TOMATOES WITH TOMATO SAUCE

1775
01:07:59,575 --> 01:08:00,443
COMPLETED 4 SAUCES

1776
01:08:00,509 --> 01:08:02,244
The salt flew backward.

1777
01:08:03,112 --> 01:08:03,946
MISS

1778
01:08:04,880 --> 01:08:06,782
THAT WAS THE MOMENT I DREAMT OF SEEING…

1779
01:08:07,016 --> 01:08:07,917
I'M SORRY, DAE-MYUNG!

1780
01:08:07,983 --> 01:08:09,585
LET ME DO IT OVER!

1781
01:08:09,652 --> 01:08:10,486
MISS

1782
01:08:11,020 --> 01:08:12,354
Nothing got in.

1783
01:08:12,421 --> 01:08:14,256
I think he seasoned the kimchi fried rice.

1784
01:08:14,323 --> 01:08:17,293
Make sure you taste the fried rice.
He seasoned that instead.

1785
01:08:17,359 --> 01:08:18,594
4. COOK KIMCHI AND BEEF WITH RICE

1786
01:08:18,661 --> 01:08:21,797
He's even adding kimchi juice!

1787
01:08:22,565 --> 01:08:24,200
Where did you get kimchi from back then?

1788
01:08:24,266 --> 01:08:27,269
There was a Korean supermarket,
so I bought it there.

1789
01:08:27,336 --> 01:08:28,838
So there was a way to get them.

1790
01:08:29,238 --> 01:08:30,639
SHRIMP

1791
01:08:30,706 --> 01:08:32,575
-What's that?
-What are you doing, Chef Hyun-suk?

1792
01:08:32,641 --> 01:08:36,378
He's putting a stick into a shrimp
to keep it straight while it cooks.

1793
01:08:37,213 --> 01:08:39,248
-To make sure it doesn't bend.
-Vertically?

1794
01:08:39,315 --> 01:08:42,318
That's how we cook shrimp for sushi.

1795
01:08:43,419 --> 01:08:46,055
-Webfoot octopus.
-Even webfoot octopus?

1796
01:08:46,122 --> 01:08:48,157
When I just started my culinary school,

1797
01:08:48,224 --> 01:08:51,127
I often went to his restaurant,
and he always had the craziest dishes.

1798
01:08:51,193 --> 01:08:52,261
He did.

1799
01:08:52,328 --> 01:08:54,830
There was <i>tsuyu</i> dip pasta,

1800
01:08:54,897 --> 01:08:56,732
sushi-shaped pasta,

1801
01:08:56,799 --> 01:08:58,100
-also,
-Sushi-shaped?

1802
01:08:58,167 --> 01:09:02,371
…he tried matching caviar, bitter lettuce,
<i>mukeunji,</i> and other unusual mixes.

1803
01:09:02,438 --> 01:09:04,306
He used to make lots of creative pasta.

1804
01:09:04,373 --> 01:09:07,409
He must've been full of passion
when he first opened his business

1805
01:09:07,476 --> 01:09:09,712
as the head chef.

1806
01:09:09,778 --> 01:09:11,480
He did his best, but all he got was,

1807
01:09:11,547 --> 01:09:14,016
"He's doing the same thing
as he did at La Cucina."

1808
01:09:14,083 --> 01:09:16,619
"It's good, but it's just La Cucina."

1809
01:09:16,685 --> 01:09:18,387
That must've killed his pride.

1810
01:09:18,454 --> 01:09:21,590
-This is a significant dish for him.
-It is.

1811
01:09:21,657 --> 01:09:23,926
-He's grabbing yogurt.
-Yes.

1812
01:09:23,993 --> 01:09:24,994
And he uses it to…

1813
01:09:25,060 --> 01:09:26,629
-Yogurt?
-For the burrito.

1814
01:09:26,695 --> 01:09:28,197
-It's the sauce.
-The burrito sauce?

1815
01:09:28,430 --> 01:09:29,465
AH-HA

1816
01:09:29,532 --> 01:09:31,033
The dipping sauce.

1817
01:09:31,100 --> 01:09:35,237
Chef Jong-won also made
a similar yogurt sauce earlier.

1818
01:09:35,304 --> 01:09:36,205
Did they know the same person?

1819
01:09:36,272 --> 01:09:38,007
Were they the same person?

1820
01:09:38,073 --> 01:09:39,742
-No, his friend was called José.
-Wait.

1821
01:09:39,808 --> 01:09:42,611
Sanchez did mention
a friend named Martinez,

1822
01:09:43,445 --> 01:09:46,182
-and I heard he had a Korean friend.
-Hey!

1823
01:09:46,248 --> 01:09:48,250
Hey! Why have you changed?

1824
01:09:48,317 --> 01:09:49,552
ENTERTAINED

1825
01:09:49,618 --> 01:09:51,954
And that Korean friend
made a mean kimchi fried rice.

1826
01:09:52,021 --> 01:09:53,589
Hey, that's totally him!

1827
01:09:53,956 --> 01:09:56,091
THE PUZZLE HAS BEEN SOLVED!

1828
01:09:56,158 --> 01:09:57,326
GETTING CHILLS!

1829
01:09:57,393 --> 01:09:59,562
He said, "Hui-tae, Hui-tae,"
and I didn't know what it was.

1830
01:09:59,628 --> 01:10:01,297
-That's his Korean name!
-It was Chef Sam.

1831
01:10:01,363 --> 01:10:03,032
Why has he changed?

1832
01:10:03,098 --> 01:10:04,500
You two had a mutual friend.

1833
01:10:05,234 --> 01:10:06,235
SNAPS BACK TO REALITY

1834
01:10:06,302 --> 01:10:07,436
These tales literally never end.

1835
01:10:07,503 --> 01:10:09,605
I love it
when the chefs jab at each other.

1836
01:10:10,005 --> 01:10:12,241
CAN YOU STOP ME?

1837
01:10:13,008 --> 01:10:14,843
-He's making a burrito.
-He is.

1838
01:10:14,910 --> 01:10:16,412
NEXT, THE SIDE SALSA

1839
01:10:16,478 --> 01:10:17,479
Lemon juice.

1840
01:10:17,546 --> 01:10:19,048
Olive oil.

1841
01:10:20,049 --> 01:10:21,951
There's not much time left.

1842
01:10:22,017 --> 01:10:24,153
AVOCADO

1843
01:10:24,753 --> 01:10:26,622
I'm glad I had ripe avocados.

1844
01:10:26,689 --> 01:10:29,425
6. MIX WITH LEMON JUICE
AND MAKE TOMATO AVOCADO SALSA

1845
01:10:30,259 --> 01:10:32,561
CHECKING THE CAPELLINI

1846
01:10:33,629 --> 01:10:36,232
Oh, this capellini is too thick.

1847
01:10:36,298 --> 01:10:38,667
How long
do you usually cook capellini for?

1848
01:10:38,734 --> 01:10:40,569
About three or four minutes.

1849
01:10:40,636 --> 01:10:43,439
It only took two minutes
for the one I used.

1850
01:10:43,505 --> 01:10:44,540
These are quite thick.

1851
01:10:44,607 --> 01:10:47,343
-I see. Seven minutes left.
-All right.

1852
01:10:47,409 --> 01:10:48,677
You're halfway done.

1853
01:10:48,744 --> 01:10:50,212
Will you wash it in cold water?

1854
01:10:50,279 --> 01:10:51,847
-I have to.
-You do? Then leave it…

1855
01:10:51,914 --> 01:10:53,549
I said that it was cold capellini.

1856
01:10:53,616 --> 01:10:55,050
Why weren't you listening?

1857
01:10:55,351 --> 01:10:56,685
DUMBFOUNDED

1858
01:10:56,752 --> 01:10:58,254
-My gosh.
-He's scary.

1859
01:10:58,320 --> 01:11:00,756
That grandpa is scary when he's mad.

1860
01:11:00,823 --> 01:11:04,727
<i>-He can be.</i>
<i>-I can't remember everything I hear.</i>

1861
01:11:04,793 --> 01:11:08,364
He'll have to cook it longer
because it'll get firmer in cold water.

1862
01:11:08,430 --> 01:11:10,966
-Is that so?
-Yes.

1863
01:11:11,033 --> 01:11:12,334
He sprinkled it on his head.

1864
01:11:13,535 --> 01:11:15,504
TIME TO COOK THE MEAT!

1865
01:11:15,804 --> 01:11:17,706
DROPPING THE CILANTRO SIRLOIN MIX!

1866
01:11:17,773 --> 01:11:19,775
His kimchi fried rice is almost done.

1867
01:11:19,842 --> 01:11:22,611
You have less than six minutes.

1868
01:11:22,678 --> 01:11:24,980
7. ADD CILANTRO TO KIMCHI FRIED RICE

1869
01:11:25,047 --> 01:11:26,515
Is that cilantro?

1870
01:11:26,582 --> 01:11:27,750
Charlo liked cilantro,

1871
01:11:27,816 --> 01:11:30,419
-so I added a lot for him.
-Cilantro?

1872
01:11:30,486 --> 01:11:33,355
-You've added cilantro? That's new.
-That's new.

1873
01:11:33,422 --> 01:11:34,390
Did Charlo like that?

1874
01:11:34,456 --> 01:11:35,824
He did.

1875
01:11:36,659 --> 01:11:38,460
I think Dae-myung will like it more.

1876
01:11:38,527 --> 01:11:40,562
-Right.
-Not Charlo.

1877
01:11:40,629 --> 01:11:42,998
I think you're leaning toward
something Dae-myung would like.

1878
01:11:43,065 --> 01:11:46,735
-The lamb <i>hambagu</i> had cilantro in it too.
-It did!

1879
01:11:46,802 --> 01:11:48,237
You liked it with so much cilantro.

1880
01:11:48,304 --> 01:11:51,206
It felt like the cilantro
caught the lamb as it tried to run off.

1881
01:11:52,341 --> 01:11:54,910
-I think it'll taste like that.
-That story is so suspicious.

1882
01:11:54,977 --> 01:11:57,212
Is Charlo a real person?

1883
01:11:57,279 --> 01:11:59,615
-He is a real person!
-Is he really?

1884
01:11:59,682 --> 01:12:01,350
-He is.
-When I eat,

1885
01:12:01,417 --> 01:12:02,918
-can I talk to him on the phone?
-Phone?

1886
01:12:03,452 --> 01:12:06,088
-He wants to talk to him on the phone.
-Can you call him?

1887
01:12:06,155 --> 01:12:08,757
-I can.
-If you can, make it a video call.

1888
01:12:08,824 --> 01:12:10,259
-Five minutes.
-Five minutes left!

1889
01:12:10,326 --> 01:12:12,328
Please report from the kitchen, Jung-hwan.

1890
01:12:12,394 --> 01:12:16,598
Which one of their stories
will end up capturing his heart?

1891
01:12:16,665 --> 01:12:18,801
Does his friend actually exist?

1892
01:12:18,867 --> 01:12:21,337
Burrata! He's using burrata!

1893
01:12:22,738 --> 01:12:24,673
Rrata, rrata, burrata.

1894
01:12:24,740 --> 01:12:27,409
-Burrata!
-Rrata, rrata, burrata!

1895
01:12:28,077 --> 01:12:28,977
AND HE GRABS BEEF

1896
01:12:29,044 --> 01:12:30,346
What's he doing?

1897
01:12:30,412 --> 01:12:31,413
-Cutting meat.
-Meat? Now?

1898
01:12:31,480 --> 01:12:33,215
He must be using tenderloin.

1899
01:12:33,282 --> 01:12:34,616
Is he making beef tartare?

1900
01:12:34,683 --> 01:12:36,085
Ah-ha.

1901
01:12:36,151 --> 01:12:38,320
Other ingredients are used as toppings.

1902
01:12:38,387 --> 01:12:39,688
-Right.
-I see.

1903
01:12:39,755 --> 01:12:42,558
He has a piece of raw beef,
shrimp, and even apples.

1904
01:12:42,624 --> 01:12:43,792
-It's like sushi.
-Right.

1905
01:12:43,859 --> 01:12:47,696
-Jung-hwan, try the fried rice.
-I see kimchi fried rice.

1906
01:12:47,763 --> 01:12:49,031
Would it be any different?

1907
01:12:49,098 --> 01:12:51,934
It's how Charlo liked it,
made with cilantro.

1908
01:12:52,000 --> 01:12:55,404
-Let's see if he would've liked it.
-Fried rice for Charlo.

1909
01:12:55,471 --> 01:12:57,506
I should try it with the cilantro, right?

1910
01:12:57,573 --> 01:12:58,707
A BITE WITH A PIECE OF CILANTRO

1911
01:12:58,774 --> 01:13:01,210
It was over 20 years ago.

1912
01:13:01,276 --> 01:13:02,177
Kimchi fried rice.

1913
01:13:02,244 --> 01:13:05,047
-Does it taste like his memories?
-Does it taste star-obsessive?

1914
01:13:06,081 --> 01:13:07,850
Does it taste star-obsessive?

1915
01:13:07,916 --> 01:13:08,751
Huh?

1916
01:13:08,817 --> 01:13:10,819
It does taste star-obsessive.

1917
01:13:10,886 --> 01:13:14,556
I didn't know cilantro
would go so well with kimchi fried rice.

1918
01:13:14,623 --> 01:13:15,557
Really?

1919
01:13:15,624 --> 01:13:17,025
-It doesn't taste weird?
-I think they'd go well.

1920
01:13:17,092 --> 01:13:18,060
I see.

1921
01:13:18,127 --> 01:13:19,094
Come on, guys.

1922
01:13:19,161 --> 01:13:20,596
I'm talking about my actual friend.

1923
01:13:20,662 --> 01:13:22,464
-All right.
-I feel falsely framed.

1924
01:13:22,531 --> 01:13:23,365
But still,

1925
01:13:23,432 --> 01:13:26,135
-his story sounded a bit made up.
-It wasn't!

1926
01:13:26,201 --> 01:13:27,970
He's just making something delicious.

1927
01:13:28,036 --> 01:13:30,906
It has a unique flavor from cilantro.

1928
01:13:30,973 --> 01:13:34,376
-It's unique.
-It is? 3 minutes and 40 seconds.

1929
01:13:34,610 --> 01:13:35,944
CAPELLINI IS DONE

1930
01:13:36,011 --> 01:13:38,414
-It's out.
-Capellini is out.

1931
01:13:38,480 --> 01:13:40,916
That was the first cold pasta he made,

1932
01:13:40,983 --> 01:13:42,951
Chef Hyun-suk's cold capellini.

1933
01:13:44,653 --> 01:13:47,156
I'm sorry to say this,

1934
01:13:47,222 --> 01:13:48,724
-but I think Hyun-suk is over.
-Why?

1935
01:13:48,791 --> 01:13:49,792
He sprinkled salt on his head.

1936
01:13:51,026 --> 01:13:51,927
GETTING A HEADACHE

1937
01:13:51,994 --> 01:13:53,896
I guess it dropped on his head.

1938
01:13:53,962 --> 01:13:54,930
This is too sad.

1939
01:13:54,997 --> 01:13:56,031
He did this,

1940
01:13:56,098 --> 01:13:57,833
then his hand felt weak,
so he just let go.

1941
01:13:57,900 --> 01:13:58,767
OH, NO!

1942
01:13:58,834 --> 01:13:59,668
After doing this?

1943
01:13:59,735 --> 01:14:01,904
I see lots of salt on his head.

1944
01:14:01,970 --> 01:14:05,441
-Wasn't it dandruff?
-No, it's salt.

1945
01:14:05,507 --> 01:14:07,576
That's sadder than dandruff.

1946
01:14:07,643 --> 01:14:11,046
-He rarely tastes his dish more than once.
-Right.

1947
01:14:11,113 --> 01:14:14,516
-But he does it twice, three times now.
-He keeps tasting his food.

1948
01:14:14,583 --> 01:14:16,151
YOU MUST FEEL FULL

1949
01:14:16,218 --> 01:14:17,653
A little taste.

1950
01:14:17,719 --> 01:14:19,488
-He tastes it some more.
-He needs more pasta.

1951
01:14:19,555 --> 01:14:21,256
-It must've been salty.
-It's too salty.

1952
01:14:21,323 --> 01:14:22,858
-It must be salty.
-It's salty!

1953
01:14:22,925 --> 01:14:24,793
<i>Salty, salty</i>

1954
01:14:24,993 --> 01:14:27,062
WHY DOES HE KEEP TASTING HIS FOOD?

1955
01:14:27,863 --> 01:14:30,566
I guess they're all different.
They're all seasoned differently.

1956
01:14:30,632 --> 01:14:32,935
I guess so. That green sauce.

1957
01:14:33,001 --> 01:14:34,136
Two minutes and thirty seconds.

1958
01:14:34,203 --> 01:14:36,038
-Try that one.
-Hurry up!

1959
01:14:36,104 --> 01:14:39,875
-Let me try a strand of this.
-Right now.

1960
01:14:39,942 --> 01:14:42,077
What's important is
whether it's too salty.

1961
01:14:42,578 --> 01:14:45,781
He's one of the pioneers
of cold pasta in Korea,

1962
01:14:45,848 --> 01:14:47,483
someone who popularized it.

1963
01:14:47,549 --> 01:14:48,517
Is that right?

1964
01:14:49,918 --> 01:14:50,853
Is that right?

1965
01:14:50,919 --> 01:14:52,321
He did popularize it!

1966
01:14:52,387 --> 01:14:54,156
Yes, he did!

1967
01:14:54,223 --> 01:14:55,357
It's not too salty.

1968
01:14:55,424 --> 01:14:58,060
-The pioneer of cold pasta.
-That's him.

1969
01:14:58,126 --> 01:14:59,228
That's our grandpa.

1970
01:14:59,294 --> 01:15:00,696
He's the one who popularized it.

1971
01:15:00,762 --> 01:15:03,265
-Yes.
-The one who popularized cold pasta.

1972
01:15:04,433 --> 01:15:06,134
He's still that old Choi Hyun-suk.

1973
01:15:06,201 --> 01:15:08,170
-The pioneer.
-He's plating the dish now.

1974
01:15:08,704 --> 01:15:10,305
SAM MAKES HIS BURRITO

1975
01:15:11,006 --> 01:15:13,775
KIMCHI FRIED RICE,
CILANTRO SIRLOIN STIR-FRY

1976
01:15:13,842 --> 01:15:14,710
RIP!

1977
01:15:14,943 --> 01:15:18,247
9. WRAP BURRITO WITH YOGURT, RICE,
SIRLOIN, AND MORE CILANTRO

1978
01:15:18,313 --> 01:15:19,548
He's wrapping the burrito.

1979
01:15:19,615 --> 01:15:21,049
-Did you cut it?
-Yes.

1980
01:15:21,116 --> 01:15:23,185
-The burrito.
-With kimchi fried rice, right?

1981
01:15:23,252 --> 01:15:25,721
-It's all mixed inside?
-Yes.

1982
01:15:25,787 --> 01:15:30,092
Pioneer Choi, you need to start plating.
You don't have much time.

1983
01:15:30,158 --> 01:15:32,427
-Pioneer Choi!
-Pioneer Choi.

1984
01:15:32,494 --> 01:15:34,897
Wait, why are you
sticking out your bottoms like this?

1985
01:15:34,963 --> 01:15:36,598
RATHER SLOUCHY…

1986
01:15:36,665 --> 01:15:38,700
It's because his back hurts.

1987
01:15:38,767 --> 01:15:41,603
You had a great posture 21 years ago.

1988
01:15:41,670 --> 01:15:42,838
One minute.

1989
01:15:42,905 --> 01:15:44,973
He can't even see as well anymore.

1990
01:15:45,040 --> 01:15:46,942
-Like sushi.
-On top of it?

1991
01:15:47,009 --> 01:15:47,943
Sushi pasta.

1992
01:15:48,010 --> 01:15:50,479
Every single pasta is made with care.

1993
01:15:51,513 --> 01:15:53,415
MIX WELL TO FINISH

1994
01:15:53,982 --> 01:15:55,484
Sam finishes!

1995
01:15:55,551 --> 01:15:56,385
SAM KIM FINISHES

1996
01:15:56,451 --> 01:15:58,921
He finished with a minute left.

1997
01:15:58,987 --> 01:16:01,456
I'd like to share a story about my friend.

1998
01:16:01,723 --> 01:16:02,925
LAUGHING

1999
01:16:02,991 --> 01:16:06,194
He really is my friend,
and I finished early to explain.

2000
01:16:06,261 --> 01:16:08,497
He really loved it
whenever I made this for him.

2001
01:16:09,765 --> 01:16:13,502
-Forty seconds.
-Forty seconds. You have enough time.

2002
01:16:13,569 --> 01:16:15,437
On top of the cold pasta…

2003
01:16:15,504 --> 01:16:17,539
-It's all different.
-Four different flavors.

2004
01:16:17,606 --> 01:16:19,908
-Seafood and…
-He topped it with matching garnish.

2005
01:16:19,975 --> 01:16:21,777
He made 4 flavors in 15 minutes.

2006
01:16:21,843 --> 01:16:23,946
-Burrata.
-He made four different flavors?

2007
01:16:24,012 --> 01:16:28,250
-He also seasoned the pastas differently.
-He did.

2008
01:16:28,317 --> 01:16:32,020
Sam serves four burrito pieces,
and Hyun-suk serves four different pastas.

2009
01:16:32,087 --> 01:16:32,955
Four different ones?

2010
01:16:33,021 --> 01:16:34,456
Are you topping this with raw meat?

2011
01:16:34,523 --> 01:16:37,259
-It's raw? Ten. Nine.
-Right. A torch.

2012
01:16:37,326 --> 01:16:39,795
-Eight. Seven. Six.
-Eight. Seven.

2013
01:16:39,861 --> 01:16:41,930
BROILS THE TENDERLOIN!

2014
01:16:42,531 --> 01:16:45,167
Three. Two. One.

2015
01:16:45,233 --> 01:16:47,202
Time's up!

2016
01:16:47,269 --> 01:16:50,405
-Hyun-suk finishes too.
-Chef Hyun-suk's done too.

2017
01:16:50,472 --> 01:16:52,140
His cold capellini.

2018
01:16:52,207 --> 01:16:56,778
Now that we know the backstories,
these dishes look even better.

2019
01:16:56,845 --> 01:16:58,380
Wait, the presentation…

2020
01:16:59,815 --> 01:17:02,184
He did that on purpose
to make it seem like the old times.

2021
01:17:02,250 --> 01:17:03,652
Yes, like the old days.

2022
01:17:03,719 --> 01:17:05,454
It would be weird
if he served this without a story.

2023
01:17:05,520 --> 01:17:07,589
-It's more like my dish.
-Yes.

2024
01:17:07,656 --> 01:17:08,991
Yes, it's exactly like
what we had back then.

2025
01:17:09,057 --> 01:17:11,059
-Like those times.
-Yes.

2026
01:17:11,126 --> 01:17:15,297
Both dishes were finished for Dae-myung.

2027
01:17:15,364 --> 01:17:18,967
First, try the kimchi fried rice burrito
Sam made, thinking of Charlo.

2028
01:17:19,034 --> 01:17:20,836
YOGURT SAUCE

2029
01:17:20,902 --> 01:17:23,438
TOMATO AVOCADO SALSA

2030
01:17:24,539 --> 01:17:29,544
KIMCHI FRIED RICE BURRITO
THAT HOLDS MEMORIES WITH CHARLO

2031
01:17:31,380 --> 01:17:35,283
CHEF SAM KIM'S TAILS OF MEXICAN TALES

2032
01:17:35,350 --> 01:17:36,818
How should I eat it?

2033
01:17:36,885 --> 01:17:39,021
Grab a piece,

2034
01:17:39,087 --> 01:17:40,956
top it with some tomato and avocado salsa,

2035
01:17:41,023 --> 01:17:44,559
-and add sauce, then eat it in one bite.
-Okay.

2036
01:17:44,626 --> 01:17:45,627
It looks so good.

2037
01:17:45,694 --> 01:17:46,828
Did Charlo eat it like that?

2038
01:17:46,895 --> 01:17:49,331
I told him to eat it like that.

2039
01:17:49,398 --> 01:17:51,133
It's just like how Charlo ate it.

2040
01:17:51,199 --> 01:17:52,701
One second.

2041
01:17:52,768 --> 01:17:54,036
-What's up?
-The passport.

2042
01:17:54,102 --> 01:17:56,204
-I still have it.
-There's his passport again.

2043
01:17:56,271 --> 01:17:58,473
-My gosh.
-He couldn't enter Mexico before.

2044
01:17:58,540 --> 01:18:00,942
-I might end up going to America now.
-Right.

2045
01:18:02,210 --> 01:18:04,713
Can it bring him back to 2002?

2046
01:18:05,247 --> 01:18:07,215
I think he likes to show off his passport.

2047
01:18:07,282 --> 01:18:08,717
PFFT

2048
01:18:08,784 --> 01:18:10,352
Like, "I've traveled a lot."

2049
01:18:10,886 --> 01:18:12,521
I think the kimchi fried rice
would stand out.

2050
01:18:12,587 --> 01:18:14,089
I think they'll go well.

2051
01:18:14,856 --> 01:18:16,491
This is new.

2052
01:18:17,159 --> 01:18:19,261
It doesn't taste like
a mix of two different dishes,

2053
01:18:19,327 --> 01:18:21,463
but a single dish
that has always been like this.

2054
01:18:21,530 --> 01:18:23,999
-As if that's an actual dish?
-As if that's the case.

2055
01:18:24,066 --> 01:18:25,734
It's probably the cilantro.

2056
01:18:25,801 --> 01:18:29,137
My Mexican friends
all loved cilantro a lot,

2057
01:18:29,204 --> 01:18:32,507
and kimchi fried rice
can be too sour for them.

2058
01:18:32,574 --> 01:18:35,277
But they enjoyed it when I made this
with cilantro and avocados.

2059
01:18:35,343 --> 01:18:36,478
PERFECT FOR MEXICANS

2060
01:18:36,545 --> 01:18:39,281
When I think back on the time
he made this,

2061
01:18:39,347 --> 01:18:41,550
they were
two foreign nationals in America,

2062
01:18:41,616 --> 01:18:43,251
working hard to achieve their dreams.

2063
01:18:43,318 --> 01:18:44,419
My gosh.

2064
01:18:44,486 --> 01:18:47,255
-They must've had many ups and downs.
-While supporting each other.

2065
01:18:47,322 --> 01:18:50,125
He put the two dishes
they would've craved the most into one.

2066
01:18:50,192 --> 01:18:51,359
You're right.

2067
01:18:51,426 --> 01:18:52,661
It's an epic.

2068
01:18:52,728 --> 01:18:55,864
-It's a union of Korea and Mexico.
-It is.

2069
01:18:55,931 --> 01:18:57,232
-Korea-Mexico.
-Korea-Mexico.

2070
01:18:57,299 --> 01:19:01,636
And in the center of that is the hero
of the 2002 World Cup, Jung-hwan.

2071
01:19:01,703 --> 01:19:03,171
He's the guy!

2072
01:19:03,238 --> 01:19:05,407
That's how the story gets tied together.

2073
01:19:05,474 --> 01:19:08,009
-We're all in this universe.
-You're right.

2074
01:19:08,076 --> 01:19:10,178
In a way, I made Sam.

2075
01:19:10,245 --> 01:19:12,647
It was you. You made him.

2076
01:19:14,616 --> 01:19:17,652
His tale continues to grow,
the tails of his tale.

2077
01:19:17,719 --> 01:19:19,354
When you listen to other people's stories,

2078
01:19:19,421 --> 01:19:21,356
you're ultimately a part of it too.

2079
01:19:21,423 --> 01:19:22,357
-Hey, Poong.
-Yes?

2080
01:19:22,424 --> 01:19:24,025
Your bangs are getting too curly.

2081
01:19:24,092 --> 01:19:25,861
-Your bangs are getting too curly.
-They are?

2082
01:19:25,927 --> 01:19:27,629
He's exhausted.

2083
01:19:27,696 --> 01:19:30,398
-I think it might rain tomorrow.
-Rain?

2084
01:19:30,832 --> 01:19:32,200
LOOKS IT UP

2085
01:19:32,267 --> 01:19:33,668
You're giving Sanchez vibes.

2086
01:19:34,770 --> 01:19:37,038
-It will rain tomorrow.
-I told you.

2087
01:19:37,105 --> 01:19:38,340
It'll rain tomorrow.

2088
01:19:38,406 --> 01:19:40,008
WEATHER FORECASTING BANGS

2089
01:19:40,075 --> 01:19:41,743
In 2002,

2090
01:19:41,810 --> 01:19:43,979
-I was near the City Hall.
-You were there?

2091
01:19:44,045 --> 01:19:46,047
Yes, there was a huge crowd,

2092
01:19:46,114 --> 01:19:48,617
-climbing up on the bus and all.
-Yes, people did that.

2093
01:19:48,683 --> 01:19:49,951
But I was a third-time exam repeater.

2094
01:19:50,652 --> 01:19:51,620
You're a third-timer?

2095
01:19:51,686 --> 01:19:52,788
I did too!

2096
01:19:52,854 --> 01:19:54,356
I repeated the college exam five times.

2097
01:19:54,956 --> 01:19:56,224
APOLOGIZES IMMEDIATELY

2098
01:19:56,291 --> 01:19:57,659
Five times?

2099
01:19:57,726 --> 01:19:59,628
And back then, I was greatly encouraged.

2100
01:20:00,529 --> 01:20:01,830
-I'm glad.
-I'm sure

2101
01:20:01,897 --> 01:20:04,399
-it was the same for Sam and his friend.
-You're right.

2102
01:20:04,466 --> 01:20:07,536
I spent the night up cheering for Korea
at a Korean supermarket.

2103
01:20:07,602 --> 01:20:09,304
You gathered at a Korean supermarket?

2104
01:20:09,371 --> 01:20:12,607
-And Charlo came with you?
-Yes, he joined me to root for Korea.

2105
01:20:12,674 --> 01:20:14,075
-Gosh.
-Gosh.

2106
01:20:14,142 --> 01:20:15,911
It's such a heartfelt story.

2107
01:20:16,878 --> 01:20:18,446
He just cried.

2108
01:20:18,513 --> 01:20:20,415
Dae-myung wipes his tears off.

2109
01:20:20,482 --> 01:20:22,851
Chef Hyun-suk doesn't look so pleased.

2110
01:20:22,918 --> 01:20:25,320
FULL OF WORRIES

2111
01:20:25,387 --> 01:20:27,589
The cold pasta.
Why didn't you finish first?

2112
01:20:27,656 --> 01:20:30,158
MARGHERITA CAPELLINI

2113
01:20:30,225 --> 01:20:32,494
BASIL PESTO CAPELLINI

2114
01:20:32,561 --> 01:20:35,130
OLIVE ANCHOVY CAPELLINI

2115
01:20:35,197 --> 01:20:37,933
TRUFFLE SOY SAUCE CAPELLINI

2116
01:20:39,134 --> 01:20:42,804
CHEF CHOI HYUN-SUK'S
ANNALS OF THE CHOI HYUN-SUK DYNASTY

2117
01:20:42,871 --> 01:20:45,574
The order of the pasta isn't random.

2118
01:20:45,640 --> 01:20:48,743
They're in the order of creation.

2119
01:20:48,810 --> 01:20:50,312
He had a reason there too?

2120
01:20:50,378 --> 01:20:51,546
-There's a story.
-Yes.

2121
01:20:51,613 --> 01:20:53,782
Start from the left and go right.

2122
01:20:53,849 --> 01:20:56,751
-The meat pasta is the last one?
-It is.

2123
01:20:56,818 --> 01:20:59,087
-This is his life on a plate.
-Yes.

2124
01:20:59,154 --> 01:21:00,255
-Yes.
-Thank you.

2125
01:21:00,322 --> 01:21:02,357
<i>It's the first page of his annals.</i>

2126
01:21:02,424 --> 01:21:06,561
<i>-Chapter one.</i>
<i>-He opened his own restaurant 21 years ago</i>

2127
01:21:06,628 --> 01:21:09,231
after leaving La Cucina
and created this cold pasta.

2128
01:21:09,297 --> 01:21:10,832
WHAT'S HIS FIRST CAPELLINI LIKE?

2129
01:21:10,899 --> 01:21:12,567
Choi Hyun-suk.

2130
01:21:12,634 --> 01:21:13,969
-Chapter one.
-Chapter one.

2131
01:21:14,035 --> 01:21:15,537
CHAPTER 1 OF THE ANNALS

2132
01:21:15,604 --> 01:21:18,039
How can I put this?
It tastes very adamant.

2133
01:21:18,106 --> 01:21:19,441
-Adamant?
-Adamant?

2134
01:21:19,507 --> 01:21:21,977
It's both sour yet creamy.

2135
01:21:22,043 --> 01:21:23,478
Quite youthful and passionate.

2136
01:21:24,980 --> 01:21:26,114
Back then,

2137
01:21:26,181 --> 01:21:28,617
my teacher didn't like it
when we tampered with the recipe.

2138
01:21:28,683 --> 01:21:31,720
He thought adding new ideas
would make the dish a joke,

2139
01:21:31,786 --> 01:21:35,257
-so I made it and served it in secret.
-In secret?

2140
01:21:35,323 --> 01:21:37,792
-It's a simple tomato and basil pasta.
-The essence of his youth.

2141
01:21:37,859 --> 01:21:39,160
-The flavor of youth.
-That vigor.

2142
01:21:39,227 --> 01:21:41,363
But he's not allowed to do that, is he?

2143
01:21:41,429 --> 01:21:42,764
-He can't.
-He would've been berated.

2144
01:21:42,831 --> 01:21:45,133
There was a chef's special on the menu
that changed every day,

2145
01:21:45,200 --> 01:21:46,368
so I took my freedom with that.

2146
01:21:46,434 --> 01:21:48,136
-When his heart burned with passion.
-Yes.

2147
01:21:48,203 --> 01:21:51,072
After that, I began to take
that creative journey.

2148
01:21:51,139 --> 01:21:52,307
And now, chapter two.

2149
01:21:52,374 --> 01:21:56,444
<i>Chapter two, cold basil pesto capellini.</i>

2150
01:21:56,511 --> 01:21:59,547
-Chapter two, basil pesto.
-This is where the journey began.

2151
01:21:59,614 --> 01:22:02,183
He opened up his epoch with this one.

2152
01:22:02,817 --> 01:22:03,985
Chapter two.

2153
01:22:04,552 --> 01:22:05,654
It's shocking.

2154
01:22:05,720 --> 01:22:07,455
-Shocking?
-Crazy.

2155
01:22:07,522 --> 01:22:09,791
I can sense the shock
when a pasta like this first came out.

2156
01:22:09,858 --> 01:22:11,092
Can you feel it?

2157
01:22:11,159 --> 01:22:15,130
-Basil pesto wasn't well known back then.
-That's true.

2158
01:22:15,196 --> 01:22:17,265
I tried making basil pesto pasta at home,

2159
01:22:17,332 --> 01:22:19,167
but I've never had anything like this.

2160
01:22:19,234 --> 01:22:20,735
It's not easy to do.

2161
01:22:20,802 --> 01:22:23,071
And Hyun-suk was the one who made it.

2162
01:22:23,138 --> 01:22:25,507
He's getting emotional.

2163
01:22:25,874 --> 01:22:27,776
MASTER CHOI!

2164
01:22:27,842 --> 01:22:29,611
Why is he low-key annoying?

2165
01:22:29,678 --> 01:22:31,346
He remembers the pain and agony.

2166
01:22:31,413 --> 01:22:33,248
-Did he poke his eyes?
-It's all coming back.

2167
01:22:33,815 --> 01:22:35,617
HIS WHITES TURNED RED

2168
01:22:35,684 --> 01:22:37,719
<i>-Now, chapter three.</i>
<i>-Year 2015.</i>

2169
01:22:37,786 --> 01:22:39,521
<i>-Cold anchovy capellini.</i>
<i>-Chapter three means…</i>

2170
01:22:39,587 --> 01:22:41,489
The beginning of <i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>

2171
01:22:42,424 --> 01:22:46,795
10 YEARS AGO,
BACK WHEN HE OWNED THE SHOW…

2172
01:22:46,861 --> 01:22:48,663
That was the era

2173
01:22:48,730 --> 01:22:51,800
when I became overly ambitious
and made many intense dishes.

2174
01:22:51,866 --> 01:22:54,602
Grandpa Choi Hyun-suk
with broken tear ducts.

2175
01:22:54,669 --> 01:22:57,038
-Cold anchovy capellini.
-And webfoot octopus.

2176
01:22:58,206 --> 01:22:59,908
What's chapter three like?

2177
01:22:59,975 --> 01:23:02,243
-Does he show himself?
-His own colors.

2178
01:23:02,310 --> 01:23:04,512
When people don't know much about food,

2179
01:23:04,579 --> 01:23:07,749
they think everything cooked
with olive oil tastes like this.

2180
01:23:07,816 --> 01:23:10,385
They have no idea
it's made with anchovies.

2181
01:23:10,452 --> 01:23:12,354
It shows that
he has a strong card to play.

2182
01:23:12,420 --> 01:23:13,922
-I see.
-Anchovies.

2183
01:23:13,989 --> 01:23:18,193
Who would've thought to mix
olive oil with anchovies?

2184
01:23:18,259 --> 01:23:20,195
-An Italian man.
-What?

2185
01:23:20,996 --> 01:23:22,630
SON JONG-WON, YOU!

2186
01:23:22,697 --> 01:23:24,599
He's also doing his best.

2187
01:23:24,666 --> 01:23:26,534
-Let's show him support.
-Yes.

2188
01:23:26,601 --> 01:23:29,871
<i>-The last chapter. Truffle oil capellini.</i>
<i>-The last chapter.</i>

2189
01:23:30,772 --> 01:23:32,607
So this is him now, right?

2190
01:23:32,674 --> 01:23:34,676
-The grandpa.
-The grandpa pasta.

2191
01:23:34,743 --> 01:23:37,145
It was when I tried using
many different ingredients.

2192
01:23:37,212 --> 01:23:38,847
When he made new attempts.

2193
01:23:39,614 --> 01:23:42,450
That was when he was in his prime.

2194
01:23:42,517 --> 01:23:44,152
The last chapter is important.

2195
01:23:44,219 --> 01:23:47,689
-There is this young man.
-He mentions a young man.

2196
01:23:47,756 --> 01:23:49,691
He meets a great person, gets married,

2197
01:23:49,758 --> 01:23:52,827
and does his best at work
to provide for his family.

2198
01:23:52,894 --> 01:23:54,696
Now, he has a baby to take care of.

2199
01:23:54,763 --> 01:23:56,998
He continues to live diligently,
and his girl grows up too.

2200
01:23:57,065 --> 01:23:58,133
AMAZED

2201
01:23:58,199 --> 01:24:01,102
-And then, his daughter gets married.
-Oh, right.

2202
01:24:01,169 --> 01:24:05,173
Soon, she also has a child of her own,
a grandson who looks just like him.

2203
01:24:05,240 --> 01:24:08,576
This was a dish that showcased
the ups and downs of a man's life,

2204
01:24:08,643 --> 01:24:09,811
and I can call this <i>The Husband.</i>

2205
01:24:09,878 --> 01:24:11,846
-Wow.
-<i>The Husband</i>?

2206
01:24:11,913 --> 01:24:13,581
Hey, you tied that in at the end?

2207
01:24:13,948 --> 01:24:16,317
THE REVIEWER

2208
01:24:16,684 --> 01:24:19,854
THIS IS THE STORY OF MY JOURNEY
TO BECOME THE BEST CHEF

2209
01:24:19,921 --> 01:24:22,357
I had forgotten for a moment.

2210
01:24:22,424 --> 01:24:25,427
So you should have some pasta
on Saturday, July 4th.

2211
01:24:25,493 --> 01:24:26,961
EVEN TIES HIS DRAMA INTO THIS

2212
01:24:27,028 --> 01:24:31,099
Then on Sunday, watch<i> Chef & My Fridge</i>
to make your weekend perfect.

2213
01:24:33,001 --> 01:24:36,571
You have to choose one of the two chefs.

2214
01:24:36,638 --> 01:24:37,906
<i>Press one to choose Sam,</i>

2215
01:24:38,907 --> 01:24:40,108
<i>and two to choose Hyun-suk.</i>

2216
01:24:41,276 --> 01:24:43,111
Please press the button!

2217
01:24:45,847 --> 01:24:47,649
-Okay.
-He pressed it.

2218
01:24:47,715 --> 01:24:49,784
They're only one star apart right now.

2219
01:24:49,851 --> 01:24:53,555
Which story did he choose?

2220
01:24:53,621 --> 01:24:55,156
Let's see the result.

2221
01:24:55,223 --> 01:24:56,191
Show us!

2222
01:24:56,991 --> 01:25:01,229
Food, Vibe, Chefs.
The story is as important as the food.

2223
01:25:01,296 --> 01:25:04,532
Chef Hyun-suk versus Chef Sam.

2224
01:25:04,599 --> 01:25:06,801
Will it be Choi Hyun-suk,
who made cold capellini

2225
01:25:06,868 --> 01:25:08,837
when he first opened
his own establishment?

2226
01:25:08,903 --> 01:25:12,540
Or Sam Kim, who made
kimchi fried rice burritos

2227
01:25:12,607 --> 01:25:14,742
for his Mexican friend, Charlo?

2228
01:25:14,809 --> 01:25:16,311
Kim Dae-myung's choice!

2229
01:25:16,377 --> 01:25:18,780
Which story touched him deeper?

2230
01:25:18,847 --> 01:25:21,483
Who's the winner?

2231
01:25:21,549 --> 01:25:24,486
Who's the winner? The winner…

2232
01:25:24,552 --> 01:25:25,820
CHOI HYUN-SUK WINS

2233
01:25:25,887 --> 01:25:26,988
Choi Hyun-suk wins!

2234
01:25:27,055 --> 01:25:30,058
ANNALS OF CHOI HYUN-SUK DYNASTY WINS

2235
01:25:30,125 --> 01:25:32,227
Chef Hyun-suk wins!

2236
01:25:32,794 --> 01:25:34,729
The man who popularized cold pasta

2237
01:25:34,796 --> 01:25:36,865
twenty-one years ago has won.

2238
01:25:36,931 --> 01:25:40,668
Both stories were very touching,

2239
01:25:40,735 --> 01:25:43,171
but Chef Sam's story reminded me

2240
01:25:43,238 --> 01:25:47,775
of a passionate young man's book
that was yet to be finished.

2241
01:25:47,842 --> 01:25:49,611
But as for Chef Hyun-suk,

2242
01:25:49,677 --> 01:25:52,647
he has written about his life
almost until the very end

2243
01:25:52,714 --> 01:25:54,983
-and he's now looking back on it.
-It wasn't that far.

2244
01:25:55,049 --> 01:25:57,986
It's not like he'll pass away soon.

2245
01:25:58,052 --> 01:25:59,988
He's healthy and well.

2246
01:26:00,054 --> 01:26:03,858
This was his first season,
and the second one will follow.

2247
01:26:03,925 --> 01:26:05,860
It felt like the end of the first season,

2248
01:26:05,927 --> 01:26:08,963
as if I have read a long epic.

2249
01:26:09,030 --> 01:26:10,999
It was so moving. And I mean it.

2250
01:26:11,065 --> 01:26:12,333
I see.

2251
01:26:12,400 --> 01:26:14,269
This means

2252
01:26:14,335 --> 01:26:17,572
he managed to end the losing streak
against Sam after the fourth loss…

2253
01:26:17,639 --> 01:26:18,840
-It's done.
-…through this round.

2254
01:26:18,907 --> 01:26:21,776
They also have
the same number of stars now.

2255
01:26:21,843 --> 01:26:23,444
Let's hear from him.

2256
01:26:23,511 --> 01:26:28,016
I rarely look back on past recipes
or serve them again to a guest.

2257
01:26:28,082 --> 01:26:30,952
As I looked at the very first dish I made,

2258
01:26:31,019 --> 01:26:34,155
I got a chance
to look back at where I started.

2259
01:26:34,222 --> 01:26:35,456
It must be a nice opportunity.

2260
01:26:35,523 --> 01:26:37,725
Chef Sam.

2261
01:26:37,792 --> 01:26:39,794
Cooking is all about why you started.
Don't forget that.

2262
01:26:40,428 --> 01:26:41,629
ALLERGIC TO CHEESY QUOTES

2263
01:26:41,696 --> 01:26:43,565
A piece of advice from a grandpa.

2264
01:26:44,165 --> 01:26:45,366
And you. Stay behind.

2265
01:26:46,067 --> 01:26:47,168
I have to go home ASAP.

2266
01:26:47,235 --> 01:26:49,404
Sam, you should say hi to Charlo.

2267
01:26:49,470 --> 01:26:50,872
To keep it short,

2268
01:26:50,939 --> 01:26:52,073
Charlo.

2269
01:26:52,140 --> 01:26:53,274
<i>Te extraño.</i>

2270
01:26:53,341 --> 01:26:54,842
It means, "I miss you."

2271
01:26:55,476 --> 01:26:58,112
-"I miss you."
-It means that I miss him.

2272
01:26:58,179 --> 01:27:00,815
Chef Hyun-suk rises as the winner today.

2273
01:27:00,882 --> 01:27:02,684
-Please put a star on him!
-Congratulations.

2274
01:27:03,585 --> 01:27:06,154
Hyun-suk finally beats Sam

2275
01:27:06,221 --> 01:27:08,223
and wins his sixth star

2276
01:27:08,289 --> 01:27:11,059
-from Kim Dae-myung!
-Thank you.

2277
01:27:11,125 --> 01:27:12,327
COMING UP: PREVIEW FOR
KARINA & WINTER'S EPISODE

2278
01:27:12,393 --> 01:27:13,828
Today's fridge owners are

2279
01:27:13,895 --> 01:27:15,830
KARINA and WINTER of aespa!

2280
01:27:15,897 --> 01:27:17,599
I plan to go very far today.

2281
01:27:17,665 --> 01:27:19,234
I hope our episodes are the funniest.

2282
01:27:19,467 --> 01:27:20,535
AN EXTRAORDINARY DETERMINATION

2283
01:27:20,602 --> 01:27:21,869
I can rotate my arm all the way.

2284
01:27:22,103 --> 01:27:23,705
SHOWING OFF ALL THEIR PARTY TRICKS

2285
01:27:24,105 --> 01:27:25,540
SEAL CLAP

2286
01:27:25,840 --> 01:27:27,342
CAN'T BELIEVE AESPA IS GOING THIS FAR

2287
01:27:27,609 --> 01:27:28,910
AESPA CAME PREPARED TODAY,

2288
01:27:29,177 --> 01:27:31,279
SO THE CHEFS ARE PREPARED TOO

2289
01:27:31,546 --> 01:27:32,480
WHOA

2290
01:27:32,547 --> 01:27:34,148
It'd be nice
if someone could grate the cheese.

2291
01:27:34,515 --> 01:27:36,384
AS ALWAYS, CHEF SOMEONE IS SUMMONED

2292
01:27:36,451 --> 01:27:37,485
He doesn't look happy!

2293
01:27:37,552 --> 01:27:38,920
I trusted him far too much.

2294
01:27:38,987 --> 01:27:40,288
-Oh, no.
-You trusted him too much.

2295
01:27:40,355 --> 01:27:41,990
I think Sam got you.

2296
01:27:42,056 --> 01:27:43,191
A METALLIC-FLAVORED DISH FOR AESPA

2297
01:27:43,258 --> 01:27:44,158
STARTING WITH THE KNIFE-FLAVOR

2298
01:27:44,225 --> 01:27:45,226
TO THE FIERY FLAVOR

2299
01:27:45,426 --> 01:27:46,461
A FEAST OF MANY FLAVORS

2300
01:27:46,527 --> 01:27:48,129
-It's an emergency.
-It's not.

2301
01:27:48,196 --> 01:27:49,264
Stop acting up.

2302
01:27:49,530 --> 01:27:50,565
EVEN HER WORDS ARE METALLIC

2303
01:27:50,798 --> 01:27:52,066
KARINA DOES THE 5-MINUTE CHECK

2304
01:27:52,133 --> 01:27:53,434
It's delicious!

2305
01:27:53,501 --> 01:27:54,869
HER SEAL CLAPPING NEVER ENDS

2306
01:27:55,103 --> 01:27:57,005
BUT WHAT DOES IT TASTE LIKE?

2307
01:27:59,374 --> 01:28:01,376
I'm sorry, but tell us
instead of sending signals.

2308
01:28:01,709 --> 01:28:03,978
WHO'LL BECOME AESPA'S FAVORITE CHEF?

2309
01:28:10,351 --> 01:28:12,220
-Ho-young's pretty much an actual seal.
-Wow.

2310
01:28:21,729 --> 01:28:26,501
Subtitle translation by: Eunsook Youn
